window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"][2]={"university_computer_science_en:M2-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M2-L1 – From your own PC to a publicly accessible server<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor’s: Web, servers and hosting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Understand the technical and\norganisational steps that turn a locally running program into a\ncontinuously accessible internet service – without having to set up a\nserver yourself.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: A server is not a mysterious computer to begin\nwith<\/h2>\n<p>Anyone who has already developed a program on their own computer\nknows most of the fundamental technical principle of a server. A server\nalso has a processor, main memory, storage media, an operating system\nand running programs. The essential difference is its\n<strong>role<\/strong>: a server program makes a function available to\nother programs or people over a network. The other party is called the\n<strong>client<\/strong>, meaning the requesting program.<\/p>\n<p>The word <em>server<\/em> can therefore refer to two things:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Server as a software role:<\/strong> A program waits for\nrequests and responds to them. This could be a local web server on the\ndevelopment computer.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Server as a computer:<\/strong> A device provided primarily\nfor such services. It does not have to be particularly large or\nexpensive.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>A database can also run as a server service without having to be\ndirectly accessible from the internet. Conversely, a powerful desktop PC\nis not automatically an internet server simply because it has\nsubstantial computing power.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What problem does a server solve?<\/strong> Other devices\nshould be able to use a common function or the same data store without\ninstalling all the software and data themselves. <strong>When is it\nunnecessary?<\/strong> If a tool works exclusively locally and does not\nneed shared data, a standalone desktop application may be the better\nsolution.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Local application:\n  User → Desktop program → Local file / SQLite\n\nClient-server application:\n  User → Browser (client) → Network → Server program → Data<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The second line illustrates only the principle. Whether the server is\nlocated at home, with a web hosting provider or in a data centre is a\nseparate decision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a local program to a network application<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"21-lokal-ist-nicht-dasselbe-wie-oeffentlich\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local is not the\nsame as public<\/h3>\n<p>A locally started program can display a webpage in the browser even\nthough no other computer is involved. A web server might, for example,\nbe accessible exclusively at the address <code>127.0.0.1<\/code>. This\nso-called <strong>loopback address<\/strong> refers to the computer\nitself. The name <code>localhost<\/code> is often used for this\npurpose.<\/p>\n<p>This must be distinguished from an address in the local network, for\nexample <code>192.168.1.20<\/code>. Other devices on the home network may\nbe able to access a service using such an address – provided the program\nlistens for connections there and the firewall permits access. An\naddress in the local network is therefore by no means an internet\naddress that is publicly accessible.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Own PC only:\nBrowser ──► 127.0.0.1 ──► Local service\n\nWithin the home:\nLaptop ──► Home network / router ──► PC running service\n\nFrom the internet:\nExternal browser ──► Internet ──► Accessible network gateway\n                                      │\n                                      ▼\n                                   Server service<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The three situations use some of the same application software, but\ndiffer in reachability, network configuration and responsibility for\nsecurity.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"22-ein-dienst-muss-aktiv-bleiben\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A service must remain\nactive<\/h3>\n<p>With a local tool, the user decides when to start it. An online\nservice, by contrast, should respond even when nobody is sitting at the\nserver. The operating system therefore often starts <strong>background\nservices<\/strong>: programs that run without a visible desktop window,\nwait for requests and, where necessary, start automatically again after\na reboot.<\/p>\n<p>Under Linux, such services are often managed by a <em>service\nmanager<\/em> such as <code>systemd<\/code>; Windows has a corresponding\nconcept of services. A service can fail, restart, write logs and consume\nresources. It therefore needs not only executable program code but also\nan operational framework: startup behaviour, configuration, permissions\nand error handling.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When excessive?<\/strong> An analysis used once a week need\nnot operate continuously as an internet service. It can be executed\nlocally or according to a schedule. Continuous operation is a\nrequirement, not an end in itself.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Where can a server be located? Five practical operating\nmodels<\/h2>\n<p>To begin with, five options are more important than a long list of\ncloud brand names.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model<\/th>\n<th>What do you receive?<\/th>\n<th>Typical advantage<\/th>\n<th>Who bears which burden?<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Development PC<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Own computer with a locally started\napplication<\/td>\n<td>No additional platform, easy\nexperiments<\/td>\n<td>You yourself; normally not public<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Home server<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Own hardware on your own local connection\nnetwork<\/td>\n<td>Control over device and data<\/td>\n<td>Electricity, network, hardware failure,\nupdates, access protection<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shared hosting<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Web space and predefined server functions\non a shared platform<\/td>\n<td>Usually little system administration<\/td>\n<td>Provider operates platform; customer\nmaintains application and data<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>VPS<\/strong> (<em>Virtual Private\nServer<\/em>)<\/td>\n<td>Virtual computer with its own operating\nsystem environment<\/td>\n<td>Flexible, clearly separated server\nenvironment<\/td>\n<td>Provider operates host; customer usually\nmanages guest OS, applications, updates and backups<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dedicated server<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Physical computer allocated to one\ncustomer<\/td>\n<td>High control and predictable\nresources<\/td>\n<td>Substantial operational responsibility;\nhardware work may be the provider’s responsibility<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>With a <strong>VPS<\/strong>, virtualisation software divides the\nresources of a physical machine into several virtual computers. This is\nnot the same concept as conventional shared hosting: with a VPS, the\ncustomer often manages their own operating system; with shared hosting,\nthey generally receive a preconfigured runtime environment and file\nspace. Nor does ‘dedicated’ automatically mean that the application is\nmaintained by the provider. Whether updates, monitoring and backups are\nincluded depends on the specific contract.<\/p>\n<p>With <strong>managed hosting<\/strong> or a <strong>managed\nservice<\/strong>, the provider takes over additional operational tasks.\nThis is a property of the contract or service, not necessarily a sixth\ntype of hardware. A rented virtual server can also be managed or\nself-administered. We consider the larger cloud service models in\nM2-L3.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"a-small-php-tool-as-an-example\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A small PHP tool as an\nexample<\/h3>\n<p><strong>PHP<\/strong> (<em>PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor<\/em>) is a\nserver-side programming language for web applications. Suppose an\napplication consists of a few PHP pages, a small database and an\nadministration area. Several users should be able to open it using a\nbrowser.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>With <strong>conventional web hosting<\/strong>, this may already be\nentirely possible, provided suitable PHP and database functions are\noffered.<\/li>\n<li>On a <strong>VPS<\/strong>, the same application would also run, but\nsomeone would additionally have to maintain the operating system and\nserver services.<\/li>\n<li>With a <strong>home server<\/strong>, electricity supply, internet\nconnection, router and your own hardware would add to the\nrequirements.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>dedicated server<\/strong> would often be unnecessary for\nsuch a small scope.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The simplest functioning operating model is not inferior. It often\nhas the fewest sources of error and ongoing obligations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The software layers of a simple web server<\/h2>\n<p>A server is not ‘one piece of software’, but an interaction between\nseveral roles.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operating system:<\/strong> It manages processes, memory,\nfiles, users, network interfaces and permissions. <strong>Web\nserver:<\/strong> It accepts web requests via <strong>HTTP<\/strong>\n(<em>Hypertext Transfer Protocol<\/em>, the transfer protocol of the web)\nand returns static files, for example, or forwards dynamic requests.\nCommon products include Apache HTTP Server and NGINX.\n<strong>Application:<\/strong> It contains the actual domain logic, such\nas user login, data analysis or calculations. <strong>Database:<\/strong>\nIt stores information that needs to persist. A database may be on the\nsame machine or on a separate computer.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                  One server computer\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Operating system                            │\n│  ├─ Web server                              │\n│  │    └─ Application (e.g. PHP or Node.js)   │\n│  │         └─ Database                      │\n│  ├─ Logging / monitoring                    │\n│  └─ Background services                     │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n             ▲\n             │ Network requests\n             │\n         Browser / client<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This representation does <strong>not<\/strong> mean that the web\nserver, application and database always run in the same process. They\nmay be different processes even though they use the same hardware. Later\ndistribution across several computers changes the communication paths,\nnot the functional task.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>static website<\/strong> may consist largely of unchanging\nHTML (<em>HyperText Markup Language<\/em>) and CSS (<em>Cascading Style\nSheets<\/em>) files, as well as images. A <strong>dynamic web\napplication<\/strong> additionally requires program logic that responds\nto requests. We distinguish these in detail in M2-L2.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a domain name to the correct server<\/h2>\n<p>When someone opens <code>https://example.org/angebot<\/code> in a\nbrowser, several independent things happen. The notation contains a\nprotocol (<code>https<\/code>), a name (<code>example.org<\/code>) and a\npath (<code>/angebot<\/code>). The address <code>example.org<\/code> is a\ndeliberately reserved example domain name, not a service offered by this\ncourse.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Domain:<\/strong> A human-readable name that can be registered\nand managed. <strong>Registrar:<\/strong> The company through which a\ndomain is registered. <strong>DNS<\/strong> (<em>Domain Name\nSystem<\/em>): Among other things, maps names to internet addresses.\n<strong>IP<\/strong> (<em>Internet Protocol<\/em>): Used for addressing\nand forwarding data packets between networks.<\/p>\n<p>It is important to distinguish the tasks: a domain registrar need not\nbe the same company as the DNS provider or the hosting provider. Nor\ndoes the name alone contain complete information about which computer\nserves the webpage.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Domain owner → Registrar: Registration of name\n                         │\n                         ▼\n                Authoritative DNS configuration\n                         │\n                         ▼\nBrowser → DNS resolution → Target address → Connection to server<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An <strong>A record<\/strong> in DNS can point to an IPv4 address, an\n<strong>AAAA record<\/strong> to an IPv6 address (<em>Internet Protocol\nVersion 6<\/em>). A domain can have several such records. A <strong>CNAME\nrecord<\/strong>, by contrast, points to another DNS name. The required\noption depends on the hosting architecture. DNS supplies information for\nfinding the destination; it does not normally transmit the HTML content\nof the webpage itself.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Practical consequence:<\/strong> An existing domain may be\nable to point to a new server without users having to type a different\nweb address. During a changeover, however, DNS caches and transition\nperiods must be considered. Technical and legal management of the domain\nname remains a separate area of responsibility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ports – which service receives the request?<\/h2>\n<p>An IP address does not uniquely identify which of the many programs\non a computer is to be contacted. <strong>Ports<\/strong> serve this\npurpose: numbered communication endpoints for a particular transport\nprotocol, such as TCP (<em>Transmission Control Protocol<\/em>, a\nconnection-oriented transport protocol) or UDP (<em>User Datagram\nProtocol<\/em>, a datagram-based transport protocol).<\/p>\n<p>Typical conventions on the web:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Port<\/th>\n<th>Common use<\/th>\n<th>Note<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>80<\/td>\n<td>HTTP (<em>Hypertext Transfer\nProtocol<\/em>)<\/td>\n<td>Unencrypted web requests, often redirected\nto HTTPS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>443<\/td>\n<td>HTTPS (<em>HTTP over TLS, Transport Layer\nSecurity<\/em>)<\/td>\n<td>Encrypted web access; HTTP/3 can use UDP\ninstead of TCP<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>22<\/td>\n<td>SSH (<em>Secure Shell<\/em>)<\/td>\n<td>Remote administration; need not generally\nbe public<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5432<\/td>\n<td>PostgreSQL (typical default port)<\/td>\n<td>Database access; should not normally be\nopen to all internet users<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>These numbers are conventions, not physical laws. A program can use\nother ports under suitable conditions. The important points are that a\nservice <em>listens<\/em> on an appropriate network interface, the\nnetwork permits the connection to the destination and a\n<strong>firewall<\/strong> blocks unnecessary connections.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Public destination address\n        │\n        ├─ Port 443 → Web server / HTTPS\n        ├─ Port 22  → Restricted admin access only if required\n        └─ Port 5432 → NOT open to public users<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What problem do ports solve?<\/strong> A computer can operate\nseveral network services in parallel. <strong>When irrelevant?<\/strong>\nAnyone simply reading a local webpage does not normally have to\nadminister particular port openings.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>HTTP, HTTPS, TLS and certificates – clearly distinguishing\nfour layers<\/h2>\n<p><strong>HTTP<\/strong> is the request-response protocol of the web. A\nbrowser requests a resource; the server returns a status, additional\ninformation and, where appropriate, content. <strong>HTTPS<\/strong>\nprotects this web communication through <strong>TLS<\/strong>\n(<em>Transport Layer Security<\/em>). TLS enables encrypted connections\nprotected against certain forms of manipulation.<\/p>\n<p>To allow the browser to recognise the server, the server normally\npresents a <strong>digital certificate<\/strong>. Among other things, it\nbinds a name to a public key; the browser checks its validity and chain\nof trust. The issuing organisation is called a <strong>CA<\/strong>\n(<em>Certificate Authority<\/em>).<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser\n   │ 1. Resolve name\n   ▼\nDestination address\n   │ 2. Establish connection\n   ▼\nWeb server with TLS certificate\n   │ 3. Verification / key negotiation\n   ▼\nProtected channel for HTTP requests and responses<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A well-known example of automated certificate issuance is\n<strong>Let’s Encrypt<\/strong>. The operator must demonstrate control\nover the domain name concerned. In the <strong>HTTP-01<\/strong> method,\nverification information is made available through a web path;\n<strong>DNS-01<\/strong> uses a DNS text record and is suitable, among\nother things, for wildcard certificates. These are only conceptual\nexamples – an actual set-up is not needed for our course.<\/p>\n<p>Certificates expire and must therefore be renewed in good time. The\nassociated keys also need protection. Automatic renewal reduces\nmaintenance effort, but does not remove the obligation to notice failed\nattempts.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> An HTTPS certificate does not prove that\na website is reputable or that its database has been programmed\nsecurely. HTTPS mainly protects the connection to the endpoint reached.\nIncorrect access rights, compromised accounts and faulty applications\nremain real risks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does a reverse proxy do?<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>proxy<\/strong> is an intermediary network service. A\n<em>reverse proxy<\/em> sits on the server side in front of one or more\napplication services. It accepts requests and forwards them as\nappropriate. An internal application therefore does not need to be\naccessible directly at the public interface.<\/p>\n<p>Imagine an office building: visitors first go to reception. There it\nis established which room or employee is responsible. Reception does not\ncarry out the actual specialist task, but organises access.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                         Internet\n                             │\n                             ▼\n                 Reverse proxy (HTTPS)\n                        │          │\n              /bilder   │          │ /api\n                        ▼          ▼\n                  Image service  Application service\n                                   │\n                                   ▼\n                                 Database<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>NGINX, for example, can act as a reverse proxy that forwards requests\nto internal HTTP application servers. Depending on the configuration, a\nreverse proxy may also handle TLS termination, routing based on the\ndomain name or path, load balancing, request limiting and logging.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>web server<\/strong> and a <strong>reverse proxy<\/strong>\nare not strictly separate types of hardware: the same program can serve\nstatic content and forward other requests. An application can also speak\nHTTP itself. An additional proxy is therefore not always necessary.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When useful?<\/strong> When several applications should be\naccessible under one domain, a unified HTTPS entry layer is needed or\ninternal services should be shielded. <strong>When excessive?<\/strong>\nFor a very simple website that the web hosting provider already serves\nentirely through its platform. A reverse proxy is not an automatic\nguarantee of security; misconfigurations can create new risks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Home routers, NAT and the question of public\nreachability<\/h2>\n<p>A home router connects the local network to the internet provider’s\nconnection. Devices within the home network often have private IPv4\naddresses. For outgoing connections, the router translates addresses and\noften ports. This process is called <strong>NAT<\/strong> (<em>Network\nAddress Translation<\/em>).<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000053_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic outgoing HTTPS request from a home network client through a NAT router to a web server, using example documentation IP addresses.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic outgoing HTTPS request from a\nhome network client through a NAT router to a web server, using example\ndocumentation IP addresses.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Outgoing connection through NAT; private addresses and ports are not\nautomatically publicly accessible.<\/p>\n<p>For ordinary browsing this works straightforwardly: the private\ncomputer starts the outgoing connection and the associated response can\nbe routed back. However, if an external computer should reach a home\nserver <em>from outside<\/em>, the path must explicitly be possible.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Internet user\n     │\n     ▼\nConnection at provider\n     │\n     ▼\nHome router / network boundary\n     │ Only with an appropriate inbound access path\n     ▼\nHome server on local network\n     │\n     ▼\nApplication<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>With your own public IPv4 address, suitable forwarding on the router\nmay in principle be possible. However, not every connection has a\ndirectly assigned public IPv4 address. With <strong>CGNAT<\/strong>\n(<em>Carrier-Grade Network Address Translation<\/em>), connections share\nan upstream address translation facility at the provider. Simple port\nforwarding on your own router does not then automatically provide public\nIPv4 reachability.<\/p>\n<p><strong>IPv6<\/strong> (<em>Internet Protocol Version 6<\/em>) is a\ndifferent addressing model: a device can in principle receive a globally\nroutable IPv6 address. Nevertheless, the internet connection must permit\nthe relevant connections and the firewall must prevent unwanted access.\nPublic addressability is not equivalent to secure or actually permitted\nreachability.<\/p>\n<p>Alternatives exist, such as tunnels established via an external\nserver or intermediary services. However, these create dependencies and\nin some cases new security and data protection questions. For a small\npaid online application, a suitable hosting provider is often simpler\nthan opening up the home network.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Crucial distinction:<\/strong> <em>Having a domain<\/em>,\n<em>having a public address<\/em> and <em>operating a secured service\nthat is actually accessible from outside<\/em> are three different\nmatters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A complete web request step by step<\/h2>\n<p>We now combine all the components. A visitor wants to access an\nanalysis in a small online tool. We assume a conventional web service\nwith an application and a database.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>The user opens a web address with <code>https://<\/code> in the\nbrowser.<\/li>\n<li>The browser determines the destination address; to do this it can\nuse DNS information already stored or query DNS.<\/li>\n<li>A suitable connection path to the destination is established over\nthe internet.<\/li>\n<li>The server or the proxy in front of it presents a TLS certificate\nmatching the domain name; a protected connection is established.<\/li>\n<li>The browser sends an HTTP request, for example for an analysis\npage.<\/li>\n<li>The web server checks whether a static file suffices or an\napplication is required.<\/li>\n<li>A dynamic request is, where appropriate, passed to the application\nprocess.<\/li>\n<li>The application checks inputs, authentication and the required\npermissions.<\/li>\n<li>As necessary, the application reads or modifies data in its\ndatabase. The database does not need to be publicly accessible for\nthis.<\/li>\n<li>The application generates a response; the web server or proxy sends\nit back to the browser.<\/li>\n<li>The browser displays the page or a result.<\/li>\n<li>Logs and metrics may be generated in the background to assist with\ntroubleshooting, operations and detection of abuse, designed to minimise\ndata collection.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User\n  │\n  ▼\nBrowser ──► DNS (name → address)\n  │\n  ▼\nInternet / public network boundary\n  │\n  ▼\nHTTPS entry point: Web server / reverse proxy\n  │\n  ▼\nApplication: inputs + permissions + domain logic\n  │\n  ▼\nDatabase (internal, not directly public)\n  │\n  ▼\nResponse returned via application and web server\n  │\n  ▼\nBrowser displays result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>The most important insight:<\/strong> This is <em>one<\/em>\npossible logical path, not a prescription for a particular software\nstack. For a static website, the application process and database may be\nomitted entirely. On a larger platform, several computers may be\ninvolved. A web hosting provider can also conceal many individual layers\nbehind a user-friendly interface.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Public accessibility means ongoing responsibility<\/h2>\n<p>A local tool only needs to work when its owner uses it. An online\nservice used by others takes on additional obligations.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"111-sicherheitsupdates-und-rechte\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security updates and\npermissions<\/h3>\n<p>Publicly accessible software is regularly exposed to automated\nrequests and attack attempts. The operating system, web server,\nlibraries and application need appropriate security updates. Services\nshould have only the necessary permissions. A database, for example,\nshould not be publicly accessible simply because the website is\npublic.<\/p>\n<p>Administrative access, passwords and keys must be protected. A\nnetwork port that is not needed should remain closed. An operator must\nalso decide how to detect and respond to security notifications.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"112-backups-und-wiederherstellung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Backups and recovery<\/h3>\n<p>A backup is an independently usable copy for recovery. For a small\nservice, this involves at least application data, necessary\nconfiguration and, where appropriate, uploaded files. A snapshot or a\ncopy of a running database is not necessarily equivalent to a\nconsistent, tested backup.<\/p>\n<p>A publicly accessible application can also lose data through user\nerrors, faulty updates or defective hardware. What matters is therefore\nnot only <em>whether<\/em> a backup exists, but whether a recovery\nprocedure actually works. We explore this further in M3-L6.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"113-ueberwachung-und-ausfaelle\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Monitoring and failures<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Monitoring<\/strong> means observing operational states. This\nincludes accessibility, response times, error frequency, disk space,\nprocess states and, where appropriate, unusual access.\n<strong>Logs<\/strong> are recorded events. They help with\ntroubleshooting and security analyses, but can contain personal data.\nThe scope and retention of these data must therefore be justified.<\/p>\n<p>A small service does not necessarily need a person monitoring it\naround the clock. But it needs a realistic arrangement for what happens\nin the event of failures and what availability is promised to users.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"114-datenschutz-und-nutzerverwaltung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data protection and user\nmanagement<\/h3>\n<p>Anyone storing other people’s user accounts, uploads or personal\ninformation takes on additional organisational and legal responsibility.\nThe location of the hosting provider is not the only relevant question:\ndata processing arrangements, access control, deletion and external\nservices can also play a role. Specific legal requirements belong in a\nseparate, currently verified assessment (M4-L3), not a blanket hosting\nrule.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"115-kosten-mehr-als-eine-monatsgebuehr\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Costs – more than a\nmonthly fee<\/h3>\n<p>A hosting offering may already include storage space, processor time\nor data traffic, or charge separately for them. In addition, the domain,\nextra backups, higher availability, support, monitoring, data transfer\nand especially your own working time may be relevant.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operating model<\/th>\n<th>Visible cost categories<\/th>\n<th>Often underestimated effort<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Home server<\/td>\n<td>Hardware, electricity, internet\nconnection, spare parts<\/td>\n<td>Failures, reachability, backups, intrusion\nprotection for the home network<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shared hosting<\/td>\n<td>Plan, domain, additional options<\/td>\n<td>Maintenance of own application, data and\ncredentials<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VPS<\/td>\n<td>Rental, storage, backups, data traffic as\nper contract<\/td>\n<td>Operating system updates, web server,\nfirewall, security monitoring<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dedicated server<\/td>\n<td>Hardware rental, extra services, possibly\ntraffic<\/td>\n<td>Administration and troubleshooting despite\npaying for hardware<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This is a <strong>cost model<\/strong>, not a current price list. A\nhome server is not automatically cheaper; it can, for example, be\nuneconomical due to electricity consumption and lack of redundancy. A\ncloud solution is not automatically more expensive, but it is not free\nto maintain either.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three illustrative scenarios from software practice<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"121-oeffentliches-dokumentations-und-downloadangebot\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Public\ndocumentation and download offering<\/h3>\n<p>A technical website publishes guides, screenshots and freely\navailable tools. Initially, it contains no elaborate user functions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Possible simple architecture:<\/strong> Existing conventional\nweb hosting or statically served files. Downloads and documentation do\nnot automatically require a separate virtual machine. Nevertheless, the\noperator must consider keeping files up to date, permissions and the\nsecurity of the publication process.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Visitor → Domain → Web hosting → HTML / files / downloads<\/code><\/pre>\n<h3 id=\"122-kleines-kostenpflichtiges-online-werkzeug\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Small paid online\ntool<\/h3>\n<p>A customer logs in, uploads a file, has an analysis performed and\ndownloads a result. The software requires user management, an\napplication and usually persistent storage.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Possible simple architecture:<\/strong> A web server and a\nsingle application on a suitable hosting plan or VPS, with the database\ninitially on the same platform or provided as a separate service.\nDistribution across several servers is not necessary as long as the\nrequirements do not justify it.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer → HTTPS → Web application → Database\n                       │\n                       └────────────► Protected file storage<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The technical effort here arises less from the word ‘cloud’ than from\naccess rights, data deletion, secure uploads, billing and support.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"123-online-schnittstelle-mit-lokaler-ki-verarbeitung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Online\ninterface with local AI processing<\/h3>\n<p>An application offers image or text processing. A powerful computer\nwith a graphics processor could perform calculations while the publicly\naccessible entry point is with an external provider.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Possible architecture:<\/strong> The public component accepts\njobs and places them in a queue. A protected processing service\nretrieves work and returns results. An AI computer on the home network\ndoes not therefore automatically have to act as an open web server.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer → Public HTTPS application → Job list\n                                         │\n                                         ▼\n                                  Protected AI worker\n                                         │\n                                         ▼\n                                     Result store\n                                         │\n                                         ▼\n                                       Customer<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Such a solution obviously requires clear rules for authentication,\ndata transmission, errors, retention periods and worker availability.\nThe precise organisation follows later in M4-L1 and M6-L6.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Common misconceptions<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Statement<\/th>\n<th>Correction<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>‘A server is a special expensive\nmachine.’<\/td>\n<td>Server primarily denotes a role; the\nhardware depends on the requirements.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘A VPS is the same as shared\nhosting.’<\/td>\n<td>With a VPS, you typically operate your own\nvirtual system environment; shared hosting generally provides a\npreconfigured platform.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘Anyone who owns a domain already has a\nserver.’<\/td>\n<td>Domain registration, DNS operation and\nhosting are separate tasks.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘Port 443 automatically means\nsecurity.’<\/td>\n<td>An open port 443 alone says nothing about\nsecure TLS configuration and application permissions.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘HTTPS makes a website trustworthy.’<\/td>\n<td>HTTPS protects transport, not all business\nand security processes.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘With a reverse proxy, no firewall is\nneeded.’<\/td>\n<td>Reverse proxies, firewalls and application\npermissions solve different problems.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘The database must be reachable from\noutside.’<\/td>\n<td>Normally only the application or an\nauthorised internal service communicates with it.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘A public IPv6 address automatically opens\nthe application.’<\/td>\n<td>Service binding, routing, firewall and\nprovider conditions must also be appropriate.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘A home server is always the cheapest\noption.’<\/td>\n<td>Electricity, hardware, failures,\nmaintenance and security belong in the comparison.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘A small web application needs\nKubernetes.’<\/td>\n<td>A single suitable hosting provider or\nserver is very often sufficient.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M2-L2<\/h2>\n<p>A publicly accessible internet service consists of <strong>several\nclearly distinguishable components<\/strong>: a computer or hosting\nservice, an operating system, a running program, an accessible network\npath, a registered domain name where applicable, DNS information and\nsecured web access. A web server or reverse proxy, an internal database\nand operational measures often supplement this architecture.<\/p>\n<p>The question ‘Which server do I need?’ is therefore premature until\nit is clear <em>which function<\/em> is to be operated, <em>for which\nusers<\/em>, <em>with what volume of data<\/em>, <em>with what\navailability<\/em> and <em>under what responsibility<\/em>. For a small\nPHP tool, conventional hosting may be more suitable than a self-managed\nVPS. An exclusively local application may not need an internet server at\nall.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2 –\nWebsite, web application, API and backend<\/a>:<\/strong> We will look at\nwhat distinguishes a web server from a backend, how the frontend and\ndatabase work together and how individual web requests become a usable\nonline service.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Technical review date: 03.11.2026.<\/strong> The following\nofficial or standards-related sources provide technical orientation.\nProvider prices, specific contractual conditions and legal questions are\nnot the subject of a current market or legal review.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>MDN Web Docs – How the web works:<\/strong> Client-server,\ndomain resolution, HTTP, webpages and the basic request flow. <a\nhref=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/de/docs/Learn_web_development/Getting_started/Web_standards/How_the_web_works\">https://developer.mozilla.org/de/docs/Learn_web_development/Getting_started/Web_standards/How_the_web_works<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>NGINX – Reverse Proxy (official documentation):<\/strong>\nForwarding and processing requests to internal application servers. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.nginx.com/nginx/admin-guide/web-server/reverse-proxy/\">https://docs.nginx.com/nginx/admin-guide/web-server/reverse-proxy/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Let’s Encrypt – Challenge Types (as of 12.02.2026):<\/strong>\nDomain control, HTTP-01 and DNS-01 as routes to certificate issuance. <a\nhref=\"https://letsencrypt.org/docs/challenge-types/\">https://letsencrypt.org/docs/challenge-types/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>ICANN – Domain Name Industry:<\/strong> Distinction between\ndomain registration, DNS hosting and web hosting. <a\nhref=\"https://www.icann.org/resources/pages/domain-name-industry-2017-06-20-en\">https://www.icann.org/resources/pages/domain-name-industry-2017-06-20-en<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>IETF – RFC 6598:<\/strong> Shared IPv4 address space and\ncarrier-grade NAT; limitations of public IPv4 reachability. <a\nhref=\"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6598\">https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6598<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>NGINX – ngx_http_proxy_module:<\/strong> Technical operation\nof request forwarding. <a\nhref=\"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html\">https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>For a public server that is actually being planned, specific hosting,\nnetwork and security conditions must be checked again using current\ninformation. This lesson describes the architecture, not a complete\nadministration guide.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M2-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M2-L2 – Website, web application, API and backend<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor’s: Web, servers and hosting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Understand how a publicly\naccessible website becomes an interactive online service, the role of\ninterfaces and background jobs, and why user management, state\nmanagement and access protection are architectural tasks in their own\nright.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: A website is not automatically a web\napplication<\/h2>\n<p>In <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1 – From your own PC to a\npublicly accessible server<\/a>, we established how a server becomes\naccessible. We now look at <strong>what actually happens on that\nserver<\/strong> once a request arrives. A browser might simply retrieve\na prepared information page. However, it can also operate a\ncomprehensive application that processes inputs, stores data, initiates\ncomputations or communicates with other computers. Both approaches use\nweb technologies, but they have very different requirements.<\/p>\n<p>The fundamental problem is that people want to use a function over\nthe internet without installing the corresponding software locally. The\nbrowser displays the user interface; the actual logic can run in the\nbrowser, on a server or across both. On the server side, data may need\nto be stored persistently and access by different users kept\nseparate.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Information page:\n  Browser → Web server → Ready-made HTML file\n\nInteractive application:\n  Browser → User interface → Application logic → Database / service<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>website<\/strong>, in the broadest sense, is a coherent web\npresence. A <strong>web application<\/strong> provides functions with\nwhich someone works: for example, editing a media catalogue, creating\ninvoices or analysing data. The boundaries are fluid; a website can\ncontain dynamic sections without the entire site being designed as a\ncomprehensive application.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> A simple project\nintroduction, documentation site or published collection of articles\ndoes not automatically need complex user management, its own API or a\ndatabase. Static files can suffice. <strong>When is it useful?<\/strong>\nWhen user inputs need to be processed, personal data managed or results\nsaved for later access.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three approaches: static page, dynamic website and web\napplication<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"21-statische-seiten\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Static pages<\/h3>\n<p>With a <strong>static web page<\/strong>, the files to be served\nalready exist. Typical examples are <strong>HTML<\/strong> (<em>HyperText\nMarkup Language<\/em>, a markup language for structuring content),\n<strong>CSS<\/strong> (<em>Cascading Style Sheets<\/em>, styling rules for\npresentation) and <strong>JavaScript<\/strong> (a programming language\nused in the browser). A web server sends the files, and the browser\nconstructs the display from them. JavaScript can also open menus, filter\nimages or sort data locally on a static page: <em>static<\/em> does not\nmean <em>without interactivity<\/em>; primarily, it means that the\ndelivered content need not be generated individually on the server for\neach request.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser ── GET /handbuch.html ──► Web server\nBrowser ◄──── HTML, CSS, images ── Web server\n                  │\n                  ▼\n        JavaScript responds locally<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Advantage:<\/strong> Few moving parts, often inexpensive\nhosting, a small attack surface and effective delivery through caches.\n<strong>Disadvantage:<\/strong> An additional solution is required for\nshared, persisted user modifications. A comments section, for example,\nneeds an external service or its own backend.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"22-dynamische-websites\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dynamic websites<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>dynamic website<\/strong> generates content based on data\nand requests. A content management system (CMS, a system for managing\nand publishing content) can, for example, load an article from a\ndatabase and convert it into HTML for the visitor. The output does not\nhave to be recomputed completely every time; frequently used results can\nbe cached.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → Web server → PHP application → Database\n                            │\n                            └──── Ready-made HTML response → Browser<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A blog using PHP and a database is therefore already dynamic.\nHowever, it does not automatically need a separate JavaScript frontend\nserver, microservices or real-time connections. A conventional web\nhosting provider is often perfectly adequate for this purpose.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"23-webanwendungen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Web applications<\/h3>\n<p>A web application places greater emphasis on <strong>an\naction<\/strong>: searching, editing, comparing, buying, uploading or\ngenerating results. The user initiates operations; the application\nvalidates inputs, modifies data and provides feedback. Much of the\ninterface logic can run directly in the browser using JavaScript, while\nsensitive operations and persistent data storage remain on the\nserver.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>Single-Page Application (SPA)<\/strong> often loads a basic\ninterface first and then updates parts of it without requesting an\nentire HTML document for each step. This is one possible implementation,\nnot a prerequisite for a web application. A traditional server-rendered\napplication can be equally convenient and functional.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Trade-off:<\/strong> More logic in the browser can enable\nresponsive interfaces but may increase the effort required for\nJavaScript, data synchronisation, error handling and accessibility. For\nmany applications, pages assembled on the server are easier to develop,\ntest and maintain. The two approaches are often combined.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Frontend and backend: Where does each responsibility\nlie?<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>frontend<\/strong> is the part of an application directly\nvisible to and usable by its users. It includes text, forms, buttons,\ntables, error messages and graphical elements. The\n<strong>backend<\/strong> processes requests behind the scenes, checks\npermissions, executes business rules, uses a database and communicates\nwith other services when necessary.<\/p>\n<p>A useful conceptual distinction is:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frontend:<\/strong> <em>What does the user see, and what\naction do they want to perform?<\/em><\/li>\n<li><strong>Backend:<\/strong> <em>Are they permitted to do it, how is it\nexecuted correctly and what data changes as a result?<\/em><\/li>\n<li><strong>Database:<\/strong> <em>What must be retained beyond\nindividual requests and restarts?<\/em><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>      Browser / frontend\n       HTML · CSS · JavaScript\n              │\n         HTTPS request\n              ▼\n         Backend / API\n   Validation · rules · workflow\n       ┌──────┴────────┐\n       ▼               ▼\n    Database       External service<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The distinction does <strong>not have to mean a separation into\ndifferent computers or projects<\/strong>. A PHP application can generate\nHTML, process user inputs and execute database queries – all within the\nsame application. Components only need to be operated separately when\ntechnical requirements or responsibilities suggest doing so.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000012_en.png\"\nalt=\"Optional reverse proxy as a front entry point for several backends\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Optional reverse proxy as a front entry\npoint for several backends<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustration: A reverse proxy can sit in front of several web\napplications and forward requests according to host or path. TLS can\nterminate at the proxy; the onward connection requires its own\nprotection where security requirements demand it. The diagram shows one\npossible operational architecture, not a prerequisite for a web\napplication.<\/em><\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-spreadsheet-tool-in-the-browser\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: A\nspreadsheet tool in the browser<\/h3>\n<p>The user uploads a <strong>CSV file<\/strong> (<em>Comma-Separated\nValues<\/em>, a tabular text format), selects a column and wants to see\nstatistics. For a small file, JavaScript can perform all calculations\nlocally in the browser. No server ever needs to receive the data. This\nminimises data transfer, costs little and is fast, provided memory and\nprocessing power suffice.<\/p>\n<p>If, on the other hand, several users want to save the same analyses,\nprocess very large data sets or work in sync across several devices, a\nbackend can be useful. It accepts jobs, stores results and manages\naccess permissions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The crucial question:<\/strong> Where <em>must<\/em> an\noperation take place? Not everything that can be operated through a\nwebsite needs to be computed in a cloud.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is an API?<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>API<\/strong> (<em>Application Programming Interface<\/em>)\nspecifies how one program can use a function of another program. This\nincludes permitted requests, required information, returned data and\nerror conditions. APIs also exist within a computer, for example between\nsoftware libraries. This lesson focuses mainly on <strong>web\nAPIs<\/strong> that are accessible over a network.<\/p>\n<p>Imagine a library: you do not need to know how books are stored or\ncatalogued internally. You need reliable information about how to search\nfor a title. An API performs exactly this role for software.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser or desktop tool\n         │\n         │  Request: &quot;Data for job 42&quot;\n         ▼\n       Web API\n         │  Authorisation and validation\n         ▼\n       Database\n         │\n         └──► Structured response<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An API is not automatically public. It may be intended only for your\nown browser, internal services or paying customers. <strong>The actual\nbusiness function<\/strong> can remain the same even when different\nclients use it: a browser interface, desktop program, mobile app or\nautomated AI agent.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"41-endpunkte-und-datenformate\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Endpoints and data formats<\/h3>\n<p>An API provided over the web usually has <strong>endpoints<\/strong>:\naddressable locations at which defined functions or resources are\naccessible. Example addresses such as\n<code>https://example.org/api/auftraege/42<\/code> are schematic; this is\nnot an actual service.<\/p>\n<p>Structured data is very often transmitted as <strong>JSON<\/strong>\n(<em>JavaScript Object Notation<\/em>). JSON is a data format, not an\nindependent network connection or database. For example, the response\ncould contain the following values: job number 42, status “completed”\nand the result address. Another service could use XML (<em>Extensible\nMarkup Language<\/em>) or a binary format instead.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>contract for the interface<\/strong> is crucial: Which\nfields are present? Are values mandatory or optional? How is an error\nrepresented? What does a later change to the format mean for existing\nclients? Without such agreements, a supposedly simple connection quickly\nbecomes a maintenance problem.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"42-http-methoden-und-statuscodes\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>HTTP methods and status\ncodes<\/h3>\n<p>The web frequently uses <strong>HTTP<\/strong> (<em>Hypertext Transfer\nProtocol<\/em>, the web transfer protocol). It distinguishes the purpose\nof a request by means of methods, among other things:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method<\/th>\n<th>Main idea<\/th>\n<th>Schematic example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>GET<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Read a resource<\/td>\n<td>Retrieve job status<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>POST<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Submit input, often to create something or initiate an\noperation<\/td>\n<td>Request a new job<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>PUT<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Replace the target resource completely<\/td>\n<td>Replace all settings<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>PATCH<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Make a partial change<\/td>\n<td>Change only a display name<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DELETE<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Remove a resource<\/td>\n<td>Delete your own draft<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>In HTTP, <strong>idempotent<\/strong> means that the <em>intended\nserver-side effect<\/em> of repeatedly executing the same request is\nequivalent to the effect of executing it once. <code>GET<\/code>,\n<code>PUT<\/code> and <code>DELETE<\/code> have the corresponding\nsemantics; <code>POST<\/code> does not guarantee this property. An\naccidentally duplicated <code>POST<\/code> to create a paid computation\njob could therefore create two jobs – if the application does not\nspecifically protect against duplicates.<\/p>\n<p>The server responds with a <strong>status code<\/strong>. Common\nexamples include <code>200<\/code> (successful), <code>201<\/code>\n(created), <code>202<\/code> (accepted, processing possibly still\npending), <code>400<\/code> (bad request), <code>401<\/code>\n(authentication missing or invalid), <code>403<\/code> (access\nforbidden), <code>404<\/code> (not found) and <code>500<\/code> (internal\nserver error). The response may also contain a comprehensible error\nmessage.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Risk:<\/strong> A good-looking API name does not protect\nagainst incorrect business logic. If <code>GET<\/code> secretly triggers\npaid actions or permissions are checked only in the frontend, the result\nis an insecure and unpredictable interface.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>REST: A widespread architectural approach<\/h2>\n<p><strong>REST<\/strong> (<em>Representational State Transfer<\/em>)\nrefers to an architectural style for distributed hypermedia systems\ndescribed by Roy Fielding. Among other things, it includes a uniform\ninterface, separate client and server roles, stateless communication\nbetween requests, cacheability information and a layered structure. The\nidea is to connect components using interfaces that are as\ncomprehensible and independently developable as possible.<\/p>\n<p>A simplified REST-like example:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>GET  /api/auftraege/42  → Returns representation of job 42\nPOST /api/auftraege     → May create a new job<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A resource can be represented technically in JSON.\n<strong>However:<\/strong> Not every API that exchanges JSON over HTTP\nautomatically satisfies all REST constraints. In particular, the concept\nof resources and uniform semantics should not be confused with arbitrary\nfunction calls.<\/p>\n<p>REST is not the only option. A <strong>Remote Procedure Call\n(RPC)<\/strong> conceptually invokes a named function in another system;\n<strong>GraphQL<\/strong> provides its own query language for selecting\nthe requested data. These alternatives solve different problems but also\nentail interface and operational overhead.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it useful?<\/strong> For clearly describable resources\nthat several clients retrieve or modify. <strong>When is it\nexcessive?<\/strong> When a small PHP application exclusively renders its\nown pages and does not require independent clients. A clean internal\nmodule boundary may then be more important than an additional publicly\nexposed API.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From request to response: synchronous execution<\/h2>\n<p>A traditional web request is <strong>synchronous<\/strong> in terms of\nthe interaction pattern: the client sends a request and waits for a\nresponse. Although the server may use asynchronous operations\ninternally, a response time is initially required from the user’s\nperspective.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User clicks &quot;Calculate&quot;\n        │\n        ▼\nFrontend validates the input form\n        │ HTTPS\n        ▼\nBackend checks identity, permissions and inputs\n        │\n        ▼\nCalculation / database access\n        │\n        ▼\nHTTP response with result or error\n        │\n        ▼\nFrontend displays result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>For short queries this is simple and effective. Problems arise if a\ntask takes several minutes, the network connection fails or many users\nsimultaneously request extensive calculations. In that case, it is\nusually preferable to <strong>accept, store and process the job\nlater<\/strong> – without requiring an open request throughout the entire\nwaiting period.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Background jobs, queues and workers<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>background job<\/strong> is a task executed independently of\na currently open browser request. A <strong>worker<\/strong> (work\nprocess) retrieves such jobs from a <strong>queue<\/strong>, processes\nthem and stores a result. A <strong>scheduler<\/strong> can start jobs at\na designated time, for example a daily backup or sending a report.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → API: &quot;Start analysis&quot;\n              │\n              ▼\n      Record job persistently\n              │\n              ▼\n             Queue\n              │\n              ▼\n         Worker process\n              │\n              ▼\n       Store data / result\n              │\n              ▼\n Browser queries status later<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The API can therefore quickly confirm: <em>Job accepted<\/em> – for\nexample with HTTP <code>202<\/code>. The actual work continues\nafterwards. A local application can already implement a similar pattern\nwith a database table and a separate worker process. A specialised\nmessage broker is <strong>not automatically<\/strong> required.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"what-happens-when-errors-occur\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What happens when errors\noccur?<\/h3>\n<p>This highlights the difference between a small script and a reliably\noperated service. A worker can crash partway through processing. The job\ncan be delivered again after a restart. The system must consequently\ndefine the available states, for example <code>new<\/code>,\n<code>in progress<\/code>, <code>completed<\/code>, <code>failed<\/code>,\nand how it handles retries.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Idempotency<\/strong> is particularly useful for such retries:\nif the same job is received twice, it must not simply be computed,\ndelivered or billed twice. Common countermeasures include stable job\nidentifiers, duplicate checks, database transactions and a limited\nnumber of attempts. We examine the details in M3-L5 – Transactions and\ndistributed correctness, and distributed processing in M4-L1.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational consequences:<\/strong> Queues require storage,\nmonitoring, error logs and rules for overloaded workers. Computationally\nintensive AI jobs can also incur high CPU (<em>Central Processing\nUnit<\/em>, main processor) or GPU costs (<em>Graphics Processing\nUnit<\/em>, graphics processor). A queue does not limit costs by itself;\nthis requires job limits, quotas and controlled concurrency.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Webhooks: One system reports an event to another<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>webhook<\/strong> is an agreed automatic notification over a\nnetwork: when a particular event occurs in system A, it sends an HTTP\nrequest to a previously configured address in system B. This differs\nfrom an ordinary API query, where system B would actively have to check\nwhether something has changed.<\/p>\n<p>Example: a payment platform notifies the application that a payment\nhas been confirmed. The application can then update the status of an\norder. The actual event does <strong>not<\/strong> become trustworthy\nsimply because some message contains the words “payment successful”.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Event in system A\n      │\n      ▼\nHTTP webhook to system B\n      │\n      ▼\nVerify signature / authenticity / event ID\n      │\n      ▼\nStore event → Optional queue → Processing<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Important protective measures include HTTPS (<em>HTTP Secure<\/em>,\nHTTP over an encrypted connection), verifying a cryptographic signature\nor equivalent authentication mechanism, checking the plausibility of the\nevent type and protecting against repeated deliveries. Since senders may\nretry if no acknowledgement arrives, the same notification can sometimes\nbe expected several times. A unique event ID helps prevent duplicate\nprocessing. Some providers expect acknowledgement within a few seconds;\ntime-consuming work should then be assigned to a background job.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> When no second system needs\nto be notified automatically. An occasionally used local utility can\nsimply query an API when needed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>WebSockets and other kinds of updates<\/h2>\n<p>Ordinary HTTP works well when the client makes a request and receives\na response. In a chat, collaborative interface or live display, however,\nnew events often need to appear without another click.<\/p>\n<p><strong>WebSocket<\/strong> is a protocol for longer-lived,\nbidirectional communication between client and server. Both sides can\nsend messages during the connection. The standardised underlying\nmechanism is described in <strong>RFC 6455<\/strong> (<em>Request for\nComments<\/em>, a series of documents for internet standards); the\nbrowser interface is provided as the WebSocket API.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Ordinary HTTP:\n  Browser → Request → Server → Response → Browser\n\nWebSocket:\n  Browser ◄════ Existing connection ════► Server\n             Messages in both directions<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Simpler alternatives suffice for some use cases:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern<\/th>\n<th>Main idea<\/th>\n<th>Typical use<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Polling<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Browser checks regularly for updates<\/td>\n<td>Job status every few seconds<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SSE<\/strong> (<em>Server-Sent Events<\/em>)<\/td>\n<td>Server sends events in one direction over a longer-lived HTTP\nconnection<\/td>\n<td>Progress indicators, stream of messages<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>WebSocket<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Both sides can send messages at any time<\/td>\n<td>Chat, collaborative editing, interactive control<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The type of connection does not remove the need for user permissions,\ndata consistency and error handling. Longer-lived connections must be\nre-established after server restarts and network interruptions. Many\nsimultaneous connections consume resources; at high message volumes, the\napplication needs a way of handling slow receivers\n(<em>backpressure<\/em>). The traditional browser WebSocket interface\ndoes not automatically provide this backpressure control.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> If a user only wants to\ncheck the progress of a computation occasionally, simple polling may be\nmore robust and economical.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>User accounts, roles and sessions<\/h2>\n<p>As soon as a service manages personal data, it must distinguish\n<strong>who<\/strong> is making a request and <strong>what<\/strong> that\nperson is allowed to do. These are two different tasks:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Authentication:<\/strong> Establishing which identity a\nrequest can be associated with, for instance after signing in.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Authorisation:<\/strong> Deciding whether that identity may\naccess the specific record or perform the specific action.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A user with permission to “read own jobs” does not automatically have\npermission to retrieve other people’s jobs. Even a technically correctly\nauthenticated user may not be authorised to access a particular data\nset.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"101-sitzungen-und-cookies\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sessions and cookies<\/h3>\n<p>HTTP requests are initially independent messages. After sign-in,\nhowever, a web application still wants to know that subsequent requests\noriginate from the same authenticated browser. A\n<strong>session<\/strong> is often maintained for this purpose.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User signs in\n       │\n       ▼\nBackend checks credentials\n       │\n       ▼\nSession is created\n       │\n       ▼\nBrowser receives protected session cookie\n       │\n       ▼\nSubsequent request includes cookie\n       │\n       ▼\nBackend checks session AND access permissions<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>cookie<\/strong> is a small value that the browser can store\nfor a website and send with appropriate requests. In a common\narrangement, the cookie only contains a random session identifier; its\nassociated identity and permissions are stored on the server. Another\napproach uses signed tokens that may contain information themselves.\n<strong>Token<\/strong> generally means a value used to prove or convey\nauthorisation; not every token is automatically a session cookie.<\/p>\n<p>The Open Worldwide Application Security Project\n(<strong>OWASP<\/strong>, a non-profit initiative for application\nsecurity) recommends, among other measures, appropriate session-cookie\nattributes such as <code>Secure<\/code>, <code>HttpOnly<\/code> and\n<code>SameSite<\/code>, server-side expiry and logout logic, and\navoidance of sensitive content in session identifiers.\n<code>HttpOnly<\/code> makes it harder for certain scripts to read the\ncookie; it does not automatically protect an application against all\nattacks.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> A session is an additional operational\nconcern: it can expire, be stolen, be lost after a restart or need to be\ninvalidated when permissions change. Such rules belong to the\napplication – not the web server alone.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"102-rollen-rechte-und-ressourcen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Roles, permissions and\nresources<\/h3>\n<p>A common model is <strong>RBAC<\/strong> (<em>Role-Based Access\nControl<\/em>). Instead of assigning every individual permission to each\nuser, roles such as <code>guest<\/code>, <code>customer<\/code> and\n<code>administrator<\/code> are associated with permissions. Roles are\nonly a tool; many systems need additional checks at the level of\nindividual data objects.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role<\/th>\n<th>Typical permitted action<\/th>\n<th>Not automatically permitted<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Guest<\/td>\n<td>Read public description<\/td>\n<td>View private customer data<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer<\/td>\n<td>Retrieve own analysis<\/td>\n<td>Retrieve another customer’s job<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Administrator<\/td>\n<td>Manage system settings<\/td>\n<td>Unrestricted access without a business or legal basis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Suppose an API has the endpoint <code>/api/auftraege/42<\/code>. A\nsigned-in user changes <code>42<\/code> to <code>43<\/code> in the\naddress. <strong>The server must check whether that specific user is\npermitted to access job 43.<\/strong> Simply hiding other users’ jobs in\nthe frontend is not sufficient. OWASP refers to missing checks on\nindividual objects as <em>Broken Object Level Authorization<\/em>\n(BOLA).<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"103-mandantenfaehigkeit-mehrere-kunden-ein-dienst\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multi-tenancy:\nseveral customers, one service<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Multi-tenancy<\/strong> means that a service serves several\ndistinct customer organisations or customer groups. A\n<strong>tenant<\/strong> is not necessarily a user: an organisation can\nhave multiple employee accounts. The fundamental question is: how do\ntenants share technical resources without mixing data and\nresponsibilities?<\/p>\n<p>Three simplified models are:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Shared application and database:<\/strong> Records carry a\ntenant ID. Access checks are particularly important.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Shared application, separate databases:<\/strong> Greater\nseparation of data storage, but additional administration.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Separate instances per tenant:<\/strong> Stronger separation\nand individual customisation are possible, but operational and update\ncosts are higher.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Tenant Company A ─┐\n                  ├─► Shared web application ─► Protected data storage\nTenant Company B ─┘                             (logically or physically separated)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Shared infrastructure does <strong>not automatically mean a lack of\nsecurity<\/strong>; separate databases do <strong>not automatically mean\ncomplete isolation<\/strong>. The actual access paths and operational\nprocesses are crucial. If a service is intended for just one person,\nmulti-tenancy would often be unnecessary complexity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stateless and stateful: Two terms that must be\ndistinguished<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>stateless service<\/strong> normally does not need context\nfrom previous requests that is stored exclusively in its local main\nmemory in order to process an individual request. Necessary information\narrives with the request or is loaded from shared storage. A\n<strong>stateful component<\/strong>, in contrast, manages state needed\nfor subsequent steps, such as an ongoing transaction, a WebSocket\nconnection or a database containing persistently stored changes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important: A stateless backend does not mean an “application\nwithout stored data”.<\/strong> User data can certainly reside in a\ndatabase; the individual backend instance simply does not have to\nremember the user exclusively through irreplaceable local variables.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                 Request\n                   │\n          ┌────────┴────────┐\n          ▼                 ▼\n     Backend 1          Backend 2\n          │                 │\n          └────────┬────────┘\n                   ▼\n            Shared database /\n            shared session store<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This pattern later helps with <strong>horizontal scaling<\/strong>:\nseveral backend instances can handle similar requests. However, shared\ndata storage can become a bottleneck, and not every application can be\ndesigned to be completely stateless.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>REST constraint of stateless requests<\/strong> refers\nprecisely to the interaction between client and server: each request\nshould contain the information needed to interpret it. A server-side\nsession in traditional web design is therefore not identical to this\nstrict REST model. At the same time, a REST system can of course store\nlong-lived resources in a database. <em>Communication state<\/em> and\n<em>business data<\/em> are different things.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is statelessness unnecessary?<\/strong> A small\napplication with a single instance can manage its session tables locally\nwithout issue. The advantage of independent instances becomes more\napparent with load balancing, failures and multiple servers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security and operations: An API is a public attack\nsurface<\/h2>\n<p>A web application entails new obligations as soon as other people can\naccess it. The most important rule is: <strong>The browser is not a\ntrusted place for enforcing security rules.<\/strong> It may conveniently\nprevalidate inputs; however, the server must check values, identity and\npermissions itself.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"typical-risks\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Typical risks<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unauthorised access:<\/strong> A correctly authenticated user\nmay try other object IDs. Remedy: checks at the level of every affected\nresource.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Malicious inputs:<\/strong> Unvalidated data can influence\ndatabase queries, HTML output or file paths. Remedy: appropriate\nparameter binding, context-dependent output encoding and safe file\nhandling.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Overload and cost attacks:<\/strong> An open computation\nendpoint may receive large numbers of calls deliberately or\naccidentally. Remedy: quotas, size limits, rate limiting, job budgets\nand monitoring.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Stolen credentials or sessions:<\/strong> Remedy: secure\npassword handling, multi-factor authentication (MFA) where appropriate,\nTLS, suitable session rules and traceable mechanisms for revoking\naccess.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Data loss:<\/strong> Incorrect changes, broken deployments\nand accidental deletion are not prevented by access protection alone.\nRemedy: backups and tested restoration.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Data leakage through logs:<\/strong> Access tokens, uploaded\nfiles and personal information should not end up in logs without\ncontrol.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For published services, legal and organisational issues also arise:\nthe purpose of processing, retention, deletion, contractual\nrelationships with service providers and support processes. Specific\nrequirements depend on use and jurisdiction; this lesson does not\nreplace an up-to-date legal review.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"ongoing-costs-even-for-small-software-projects\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ongoing costs,\neven for small software projects<\/h3>\n<p>Often only the monthly server bill is considered. In reality, the\ndomain, data storage, backup copies, outbound data transfer, email\ndelivery, monitoring, updates and customer support all contribute to the\ntotal effort. Background calculations in particular can incur\nusage-based costs. A small tool with few users can often operate on a\nconventional hosting plan or small server, provided the required\nfunctions are supported.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Complex architecture is not a quality criterion in\nitself.<\/strong> A modern service can also be a well-maintained monolith\nwith a database. Microservices, persistent WebSocket connections or\nseveral separate queues only make sense if they solve an actual\nproblem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three end-to-end practical scenarios<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"131-dokumentations--und-projektwebsite\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Documentation and\nproject website<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Objective:<\/strong> Make texts, images, manuals and software\ndescriptions publicly readable.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Visitor → Browser → Web host → HTML / CMS page<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If few personalised functions are needed, static output or a simple\nCMS page is often sufficient. A public API, user accounts and WebSockets\nwould create extra maintenance and security work without a corresponding\nbenefit. Operations focus on updates, availability, backups and comment\nfunctions where appropriate.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"132-kleines-online-analysewerkzeug\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Small online analysis\ntool<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Objective:<\/strong> A user uploads data, receives an analysis\nand can retrieve it again later.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → Form / display → API / backend\n                                │\n                                ├─► Database: users, jobs, results\n                                └─► Optional worker for longer computations<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A first version can consist of a single backend with a database. If\ncomputations only take seconds, no queue is needed yet. Separating the\nweb application and workers only becomes interesting with longer,\nconcurrent jobs. User accounts require a secure session or comparable\nauthentication; each analysis must be checked against its owner.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"133-ki-verarbeitungsdienst\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>AI processing service<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Objective:<\/strong> Users submit text or media and have\nresults generated from them. The AI computation runs in a local or\nrented computing environment.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser or API customer\n        │\n        ▼\n      Backend ──► Check user / plan / cost limit\n        │\n        ▼\n     Create job ──► Queue ──► AI worker (CPU/GPU)\n        │                         │\n        └─ Retrieve status ◄─ Store result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Decoupling can offer major benefits here: short web requests do not\nblock AI computations that take minutes, and worker processes can be\noperated independently. Job IDs, retry logic, storage limits,\npermissions for results and cost control become central tasks. A\nWebSocket is <strong>optional<\/strong> for simply displaying progress;\npolling or SSE can suffice.<\/p>\n<p>The essential business question is not whether such an architecture\nis technically possible, but whether demand, price, hardware costs, data\nprotection and ongoing support fit together. We return to this trade-off\nin M4-L4 and M4-L5.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision aid: start small, expand deliberately<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement<\/th>\n<th>Obvious simple solution<\/th>\n<th>Expand only when genuinely needed<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Display texts and handouts<\/td>\n<td>Static website<\/td>\n<td>CMS for frequent editorial updates<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Forms and database<\/td>\n<td>Server-side web application<\/td>\n<td>Separate frontend/API for multiple clients<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fast data computation<\/td>\n<td>Directly within request or locally in browser<\/td>\n<td>Background job for long runtime or high load<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Show job progress<\/td>\n<td>Regular status query<\/td>\n<td>SSE or WebSocket for more timely updates<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User sign-in<\/td>\n<td>Proven session management<\/td>\n<td>Central identity service for several applications<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple customers<\/td>\n<td>Clean user and data model<\/td>\n<td>Tenant architecture where organisational separation is needed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Few online users<\/td>\n<td>One server, one application<\/td>\n<td>More instances, queues and load balancing if bottlenecks arise<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The table is not a dogma. It shows a sensible order in which to solve\nproblems: <strong>Function → data → permissions → reliability → only\nthen scaling<\/strong>. The specific technology should suit the actual\nusage patterns.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M2-L3<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>website<\/strong> provides content; a <strong>web\napplication<\/strong> additionally provides functions for carrying out\nwork. The <strong>frontend<\/strong> enables interaction; the\n<strong>backend<\/strong> validates and processes operations. An\n<strong>API<\/strong> defines a contract between programs.\n<strong>REST<\/strong> is an architectural style, not simply another term\nfor JSON over HTTP. <strong>Webhooks<\/strong> report events between\nsystems, <strong>background jobs<\/strong> decouple long-running tasks\nfrom browser requests, and <strong>WebSockets<\/strong> enable\nbidirectional communication over an existing connection.\n<strong>Sessions<\/strong>, <strong>roles<\/strong>,\n<strong>tenants<\/strong> and <strong>stateless services<\/strong> solve\ndistinct problems of identity, permissions, data separation and\nscalability.<\/p>\n<p>The central insight is: <strong>A small website does not become a\nprofessional service by incorporating as many technologies as possible.\nWhat matters is comprehensible interfaces, correct states, secure\npermissions and reliably manageable operations.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>In <strong>M2-L3 – Hosting and cloud deployment models<\/strong> we\nnext ask who operates these components and which responsibilities lie\nwith the provider or software developer: traditional hosting, rented\nservers, managed platforms, serverless and cloud service models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Review date:<\/strong> 03.11.2026. The sources explain\narchitectural principles and current recommendations; they are not price\nlists or a substitute for a security or legal review of a specific\nproduct.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MDN Web Docs – HTTP request methods:<\/strong> Meaning of\nGET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE, and idempotency. <a\nhref=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/de/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Methods\">https://developer.mozilla.org/de/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Methods<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Roy T. Fielding – Dissertation, Chapter 5 “Representational\nState Transfer (REST)” (2000):<\/strong> REST constraints and\narchitectural trade-offs. <a\nhref=\"https://ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/rest_arch_style.htm\">https://ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/rest_arch_style.htm<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>IETF – RFC 6455, The WebSocket Protocol (2011):<\/strong>\nStandardised bidirectional connection. <a\nhref=\"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/rfc6455/\">https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/rfc6455/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>MDN Web Docs – WebSocket API and EventSource:<\/strong>\nBidirectional WebSockets, one-way Server-Sent Events and information on\nbackpressure control. <a\nhref=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebSockets_API\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebSockets_API<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventSource\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventSource<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>GitHub Docs – Best practices for using webhooks:<\/strong>\nAuthenticity verification, prompt acknowledgement, queues and retries.\n<a\nhref=\"https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/using-webhooks/best-practices-for-using-webhooks\">https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/using-webhooks/best-practices-for-using-webhooks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>OWASP – Session Management Cheat Sheet:<\/strong> Session\nidentifiers, cookie attributes, expiry and protection against abuse. <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>OWASP API Security Top 10 (2023 edition):<\/strong> Broken\nobject-level authorisation and other API risks. <a\nhref=\"https://api-security.owasp.org/editions/2023/en/0x11-t10/\">https://api-security.owasp.org/editions/2023/en/0x11-t10/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Microsoft Learn – Architectural Approaches for a Multitenant\nSolution:<\/strong> Trade-offs between shared and separate\ninfrastructure. <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/guide/multitenant/approaches/overview\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/guide/multitenant/approaches/overview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M2-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M2-L3 – Hosting and cloud deployment models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor’s: Web, servers and hosting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: Not where the software runs, but who takes care of\nwhat<\/h2>\n<p>In M2-L1, an ordinary computer became a publicly accessible server.\nIn M2-L2, we divided a web application into a user interface, backend,\nprogramming interfaces and data storage. The next question now arises:\n<strong>Where can such an application be operated without you having to\ntake on every hardware and maintenance task yourself?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The answer is not simply “in the cloud”. There are substantial\ndifferences between your own home computer, a conventional web hosting\npackage, a rented virtual server and a fully managed online service.\nThey concern primarily <strong>control, operational responsibility,\nflexibility to make changes, costs and dependencies<\/strong>. The same\nsmall PHP application can be deployed in different ways according to the\ncircumstances; the best choice depends on its requirements.<\/p>\n<p>This lesson distinguishes three levels that are frequently conflated\nin advertising:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Operating location and ownership:<\/strong> At home, in your\nown data centre, in somebody else’s data centre or on shared\ninfrastructure.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Service model:<\/strong> Am I renting resources, a managed\nplatform or a ready-made application?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Business model:<\/strong> Am I using a service myself or\nselling my own software to customers through it?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The most important conclusion in advance: <strong>Offering an\napplication online does not mean having to operate as a cloud provider\nyourself.<\/strong> You can use a very simple third-party hosting service\nand still offer your own commercial software.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-hosting: Your own computer as a server<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Self-hosting<\/strong> means that you provide and operate the\nessential technical resources yourself. The term\n<strong>on-premises<\/strong>, meaning “at your own premises”, is often\nused. This may be a home PC, a small office computer or an entire server\ninstallation in your own data centre. The distinction matters:\nself-hosting initially says nothing about the size of the system.<\/p>\n<p>Your own computer can make sense if data is deliberately kept\nlocally, if an internet connection is not reliably available or if you\nneed specialised hardware. A local AI computer with a graphics card is a\nclear example. An internal development environment, a private media\narchive or a local test server also fits this model well.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Local user → Home network → Own computer → Application → Local data\n                                  │\n                                  └── Maintenance, power and hardware yourself<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What does this solve?<\/strong> Maximum control over the\noperating system, hardware and local data. <strong>When is it\nunnecessary?<\/strong> When only a small public website needs to be\naccessible. The disadvantages often outweigh the benefits: electricity\nconsumption, outages, internet connectivity, lack of a public address,\nnoise, access protection and your own responsibility for maintenance and\nbackups.<\/p>\n<p>Incidentally, a home server and a <em>private cloud<\/em> are not\nautomatically the same thing. The term “cloud” typically presupposes\nadditional characteristics such as on-demand provisioning and shared\nresource management. Renaming an individual computer does not turn it\ninto a cloud.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Colocation: Your own hardware in another data centre<\/h2>\n<p>With <strong>colocation<\/strong>, sometimes also called “housing”,\nyou generally place your own hardware in a professional data centre.\nDepending on the contract, the operator provides facilities such as rack\nspace, power, cooling, physical access control and network connectivity.\nResponsibility for the actual server, its components, operating system\nand applications can largely remain with you.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Own server (your property)\n          │\n          ▼\nThird-party data centre\n   ├── Power / cooling / space\n   ├── Network connectivity\n   └── Physical access control\n          │\n          ▼\nYour software and data<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This solves the location-related problems of a home server, not\nautomatically its administration problems. If a drive fails, it must be\nclear who replaces it; a data centre does not repair your application of\nits own accord. Colocation can be useful for special hardware\nrequirements or servers you already own. For an initial small online\ntool, this would usually be an excessive starting point.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Distinction:<\/strong> With a <em>dedicated rented\nserver<\/em>, the provider supplies the hardware; with colocation, the\ncustomer normally owns the hardware. Contracts may include additional\nmaintenance services, so the specific responsibilities are crucial.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Traditional web hosting: A ready-made home for the\nwebsite<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Shared hosting<\/strong> means that several customers are\nhosted in a server environment managed by the provider. For example, you\nreceive web storage, domain integration, PHP execution, a database and\nan administration interface. The host typically takes care of the\nphysical infrastructure and substantial parts of system\nadministration.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → Hosting provider’s web server\n             ├── Website A\n             ├── Website B (your PHP application)\n             └── Website C\n                    │\n                    ▼\n              Database supplied by host<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>You can often operate a content management system, documentation site\nor small PHP application there without administering Linux yourself. The\nlimitation is that you cannot install arbitrary background programs or\nchange arbitrary server settings. Process runtimes, memory, database\nconnections and background jobs may also be limited.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What does this solve?<\/strong> Inexpensive operation of\ntypical websites with little maintenance. <strong>When is it\ninsufficient?<\/strong> When you need your own persistent processes,\nspecial runtime environments, GPU-accelerated computations, individual\nnetwork configurations or complex services.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> “Shared hosting” is neither a security\nguarantee nor a statement about which processing capacity is exclusively\navailable to you. The provider must correctly implement separation\nbetween customers. You typically remain responsible for your own web\ncode, passwords, extensions and correct application configuration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>VPS and dedicated rented servers: More freedom, more\nresponsibility<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>VPS<\/strong> (<em>Virtual Private Server<\/em>) is a rented\nserver provided through virtualisation. You usually administer your own\nguest operating system, have substantially more configuration options\nthan with shared hosting and can operate several services of your own.\nThe host normally maintains the physical hardware and underlying\nvirtualisation, not automatically your software within the virtual\nserver.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Provider’s physical host\n   ├── VPS customer A\n   ├── VPS customer B → Linux → Web server → Own app\n   └── VPS customer C<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>dedicated server<\/strong>, by contrast, is usually a rented\nphysical computer that is not provided as a conventional shared VPS. It\noffers greater control over the entire machine and potentially more\npredictable allocation of resources; the specific characteristics depend\non the offering. It is not necessary for ordinary websites, but may be\nattractive for special workloads or particular isolation\nrequirements.<\/p>\n<p>With a VPS, you typically have to plan operating system and security\nupdates, firewall settings, user permissions, runtime environments,\ndatabase operations, monitoring, backups and recovery yourself. How much\nis actually taken care of depends on whether the service is\n<em>unmanaged<\/em> or <em>managed<\/em>. <strong>“Rented” does not mean\n“administered”.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A small VPS is a reasonable starting point for many independently\ndeveloped services, provided operational experience or reliable support\nis available. For a developer who wants to spend as little time as\npossible on system administration, a managed alternative may be more\neconomical – even if its fixed monthly fee is higher.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What distinguishes cloud computing from ordinary server\nrental?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Cloud computing<\/strong> refers to a delivery model in which\ncomputing, storage and network resources are accessible over a network\non demand and can typically be provisioned and released rapidly. The US\n<strong>NIST<\/strong> (<em>National Institute of Standards and\nTechnology<\/em>) published a definition in 2011 that remains\nfoundational today. It identifies five essential characteristics:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>On-demand self-service:<\/strong> Resources can be\nprovisioned without an individual manual ordering process.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Broad network access:<\/strong> Services are accessible\nthrough standardised network interfaces.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Resource pooling:<\/strong> Infrastructure is shared among\nseveral users or organisations.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Rapid elasticity:<\/strong> Capacity can be expanded or\nreduced as required.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Measured service:<\/strong> Resource consumption can be\nrecorded and used as a basis for billing.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer → Cloud management → Shared resource pool\n                             ├── Compute\n                             ├── Storage\n                             └── Networks\n                 Allocate capacity on demand<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Not every advertised “cloud server” meets all these characteristics\nto the same extent. A fixed VPS with a flat monthly fee can function in\na technically very similar way to a cloud VM, but may be less flexible\nto provision or scale. Conversely, providers use cloud technology for\nseemingly simple hosting services.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What does cloud computing solve?<\/strong> Infrastructure and\nplatform functions become available without having to procure your own\nhardware. <strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> When a small system\nwith consistently low demand runs reliably on a simple hosting package.\n“Cloud” is not a judgement of quality or an automatic guarantee of\nresilience.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Public, private and hybrid clouds: Who can use the\ninfrastructure?<\/h2>\n<p>These terms describe <strong>deployment models<\/strong>. They differ\nfrom the service models IaaS, PaaS and SaaS discussed later.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"public-cloud-publicly-offered-cloud-infrastructure\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Public cloud\n– publicly offered cloud infrastructure<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>public cloud<\/strong> is offered as a service by an\noperator to a broad group of customers. You use its resources without\nhaving to own the data centre. “Public” does <strong>not<\/strong> mean\nthat the data stored there must be publicly accessible. Private\nnetworks, access controls and isolated user accounts are particularly\nimportant in such environments.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"private-cloud-a-cloud-intended-for-one-organisation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Private\ncloud – a cloud intended for one organisation<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>private cloud<\/strong> is exclusively available to one\norganisation. It can be operated internally or by a service provider, on\nthe organisation’s own premises or at external locations. What matters\nis that it is provided as cloud infrastructure for that organisation,\nnot merely who owns the hardware. An ordinary internal file store is\ntherefore not automatically a private cloud.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"hybrid-cloud-combining-different-cloud-environments\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hybrid\ncloud – combining different cloud environments<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>hybrid cloud<\/strong> connects distinct cloud environments,\nfor example a private cloud with a public cloud. Applications or data\ncan work together across them according to defined rules. A local\ncomputer plus any external web service is sometimes called “hybrid” in\neveryday language, but does not necessarily satisfy a formal hybrid\ncloud definition.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"community-cloud-and-multicloud\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Community cloud and\nmulticloud<\/h3>\n<p>The NIST classification also includes a <strong>community\ncloud<\/strong>, which serves a group of organisations with shared\nrequirements. <strong>Multicloud<\/strong> in current usage means using\nofferings from several cloud providers. It is not one of the four\noriginal NIST deployment models and presents its own integration\nproblems.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Practical rule:<\/strong> For most small projects of your own,\none hosting environment suffices. Private, hybrid and multicloud\nstructures introduce additional connections, identity rules, cost\ncontrols and sources of failure; they only become worthwhile when there\nare specific requirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>IaaS, PaaS and SaaS: Three levels of responsibility<\/h2>\n<p>The three classic <strong>cloud service models<\/strong> describe\n<strong>which part of the technical solution you receive as a\nservice<\/strong>. The same organisation can use several models\nsimultaneously.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000066_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of IaaS, PaaS and SaaS as layers of responsibility. With IaaS, the user is responsible for more software layers than with PaaS and SaaS.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of IaaS, PaaS and SaaS as\nlayers of responsibility. With IaaS, the user is responsible for more\nsoftware layers than with PaaS and SaaS.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>IaaS, PaaS and SaaS shift operational responsibilities; contractual\ndetails may differ.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"81-iaas-infrastructure-as-a-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>IaaS – Infrastructure as a\nService<\/h3>\n<p><strong>IaaS<\/strong> means <em>Infrastructure as a Service<\/em>. You\nrent fundamental resources: virtual machines, storage devices, networks\nor similar infrastructure components. The provider operates the physical\nenvironment and virtualisation. You typically install or manage the\noperating system, web server, database and application.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>IaaS provider: Data centre → Hardware → Virtualisation\n                                              │\nCustomer:                             Guest operating system\n                                              │\n                                      Web server + application\n                                              │\n                                            Database<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Problem solved:<\/strong> You do not have to procure hardware,\nbut retain considerable technical freedom. <strong>Unnecessary<\/strong>\nif your program can run without problems on traditional hosting or a\nmanaged platform. The often underestimated disadvantage is\nresponsibility for updates, secure configuration and recovery of your\nown environment.<\/p>\n<p>A VPS can be functionally very similar to IaaS. Whether the specific\nservice conforms to the cloud model in a stricter sense depends on\nprovisioning, elasticity and usage model; the name “VPS” alone does not\ndecide that.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"82-paas-platform-as-a-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>PaaS – Platform as a Service<\/h3>\n<p><strong>PaaS<\/strong> means <em>Platform as a Service<\/em>. Instead\nof administering an entire server yourself, you primarily deploy your\napplication. The provider typically operates the underlying runtime and\noperating system environment, takes on some of the scaling work and\nprovides tools for deployment and monitoring.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>You:      Application code + configuration + data logic\n                            │\n                            ▼\nPaaS:          Runtime + operating system + scaling\n                            │\n                            ▼\n                    Physical infrastructure<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Problem solved:<\/strong> Less administrative work; greater\nfocus on your own software. <strong>Unnecessary<\/strong> for a static\nwebsite or an application for which a conventional web hosting plan\nalready provides everything. Disadvantages may include limited runtime\nenvironments, restrictions on background processes, additional platform\ncosts and technical dependence on the provider.<\/p>\n<p>A managed platform does not remove the obligation to write secure\napplication code. It is still necessary to check whether the database\nneeds a separate backup, which process limits apply and how scaling is\nbilled.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"83-saas-software-as-a-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>SaaS – Software as a Service<\/h3>\n<p><strong>SaaS<\/strong> means <em>Software as a Service<\/em>. The\ncustomer uses a largely ready-made application through a browser or\nprogramming interface instead of installing servers, runtime\nenvironments and application software themselves. The SaaS operator\ntypically bears much of the technical maintenance responsibility, while\nthe customer remains responsible for the proper handling of their data,\naccounts and sharing permissions.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>End customer → Browser → Ready-made online service\n                         │\n                  SaaS provider operates\n             Application + platform + infrastructure<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Problem solved:<\/strong> A standardised function is\nimmediately usable. <strong>Unnecessary<\/strong> if you require entirely\ncustom software logic that the ready-made service does not offer.\nDisadvantages include ongoing usage fees, limited customisability and\ndependence on export functions and contractual terms.<\/p>\n<p>An important misconception arises here: <strong>If you develop and\nsell an online service to customers yourself, you may be offering SaaS –\neven if you have rented only a small VPS for that purpose.<\/strong> The\nbusiness model of your offering is distinct from the infrastructure\nmodel you purchase yourself.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Additional models: CaaS, FaaS and managed databases<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"91-caas-containers-as-a-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>CaaS – Containers as a\nService<\/h3>\n<p><strong>CaaS<\/strong> means <em>Containers as a Service<\/em>.\nContainers package programs together with important runtime dependencies\ninto a defined execution unit. A CaaS offering takes over part of the\ndeployment and management of these containers, frequently through a\nmanaged orchestration environment. An <strong>orchestrator<\/strong>, for\nexample, coordinates which container instance runs on which machine and\nwhen new instances are started.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Your container image → Managed container platform\n                                    │\n                               Start / manage\n                                    │\n                              Running containers<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Useful<\/strong> when several services or special runtime\nenvironments need to be deployed reproducibly.\n<strong>Excessive<\/strong> for a single small PHP website. Specific\nresponsibilities vary considerably: a platform may manage the cluster,\nwhile responsibility for container images, dependencies, permissions and\ndata remains partly with you. The technology itself is explained in\ndetail in M2-L4.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"92-faas-function-as-a-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>FaaS – Function as a Service<\/h3>\n<p><strong>FaaS<\/strong> means <em>Function as a Service<\/em>. You\ndeploy a small function triggered by an event rather than operating your\nown server process continuously. An event can be an incoming web\nrequest, a message or a new file. The platform starts the required\nexecution, monitors resources and, depending on the service, can add\ncapacity.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>File uploaded → Event → Execute function\n                                  │\n                           Generate thumbnail\n                                  │\n                               Save file<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Useful<\/strong> for irregular, clearly bounded tasks.\n<strong>Unnecessary or unsuitable<\/strong> when an application runs\ncontinuously, requires tightly coupled processes or depends heavily on\nprovider-specific limits. Factors to consider include startup delays\n(“cold starts”), execution limits, concurrent invocations, database\nconnections and usage-based charges.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"93-serverless-eine-betriebsform-kein-zauberwort\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Serverless – an\noperating model, not a magic word<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Serverless<\/strong> essentially means “without server\nadministration”, not “without servers”. The provider still operates\ncomputers, but the platform largely hides their provisioning and\noperation. FaaS is one form of serverless computing. Serverless\ncontainer and database services also exist. Some services can scale down\nto zero active instances; others have continually billed components or\nminimum capacities.<\/p>\n<p>A typical serverless application looks like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User → API entry point → Serverless function → Managed database\n                            │\n                       Logs / monitoring<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>What does this solve?<\/strong> Irregular load without\npermanent administration. <strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> For a\nsmall, steadily used application, a simple hosting package or VPS can be\nmore economical and easier to understand. Serverless does not remove\nresponsibility for inputs, permissions, data deletion, incorrect retries\nor cost overruns.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"94-managed-database-verwaltete-datenbank\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Managed database<\/h3>\n<p>With a <strong>managed database<\/strong>, the operator takes on\nsubstantial parts of database operations, depending on the product:\ninfrastructure, provisioning, updates, backup functions or monitoring.\nYou configure the database, model tables and manage queries, access,\ndata quality and usually the recovery strategy.<\/p>\n<p>A managed database service is neither automatically infinitely\nscalable nor inherently protected from every operational error. In\nparticular, <strong>replication is distinct from backup<\/strong>. An\naccidentally deleted record may also be lost in a highly available\ndatabase; recovery and retention periods therefore have to be assessed\nseparately. We cover distributed database systems in module M3.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Who bears which responsibility?<\/h2>\n<p>The term <strong>shared responsibility model<\/strong> refers to a\nmodel in which responsibility is divided between parties. It is\ndocumented in detail by AWS and Microsoft, among others. In simplified\nterms, the more infrastructure the provider manages, the less you must\nadminister yourself – but responsibility for your data and proper\noperation of the application remains.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer / task<\/th>\n<th>Own server<\/th>\n<th>IaaS / typical VPS<\/th>\n<th>PaaS / managed platform<\/th>\n<th>SaaS as a user<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical hardware / building<\/td>\n<td>Yourself<\/td>\n<td>Provider<\/td>\n<td>Provider<\/td>\n<td>Provider<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Virtualisation / infrastructure<\/td>\n<td>Yourself, if used<\/td>\n<td>Provider<\/td>\n<td>Provider<\/td>\n<td>Provider<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application operating system<\/td>\n<td>Yourself<\/td>\n<td>Usually yourself<\/td>\n<td>Usually provider<\/td>\n<td>Provider<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operating application code<\/td>\n<td>Yourself<\/td>\n<td>Yourself<\/td>\n<td>Develop it yourself; platform operates runtime<\/td>\n<td>Provider operates finished app<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Users and permissions<\/td>\n<td>Yourself<\/td>\n<td>Yourself<\/td>\n<td>Yourself, partly shared<\/td>\n<td>Own accounts/sharing permissions still relevant<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Handling own data properly<\/td>\n<td>Yourself<\/td>\n<td>Yourself<\/td>\n<td>Yourself<\/td>\n<td>Own organisational obligations remain<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backups and restoration<\/td>\n<td>Entirely yourself<\/td>\n<td>Plan yourself<\/td>\n<td>Divided depending on contract<\/td>\n<td>Check export/retention terms<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This table is <strong>an orientation model, not universal contractual\nterms<\/strong>. A managed VPS may take on more tasks than an ordinary\nIaaS server; a PaaS service may leave certain system configurations to\nthe customer. For each specific offering, ask: <em>Who patches what, who\ndetects faults, who restores data, and who is responsible if an\nautomated backup fails?<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A useful contrast is <strong>security of the cloud<\/strong> versus\n<strong>security in the cloud<\/strong>. For example, the provider\nsecures the data centre and hypervisor; at your level, you secure user\naccounts, permissions, secrets, configuration and content. The boundary\nshifts according to the service model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three different architectures for the same software\nidea<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose you develop an online tool that analyses an uploaded CSV file\nand displays an overview of the results. The functional task remains the\nsame; operationally, different solutions emerge.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"option-a-traditional-hosting-with-php\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Option A: Traditional\nhosting with PHP<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → PHP application at web host → Small database<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The host supplies the standard environment. You take care of the PHP\ncode, validation, updates to your own application and handling of user\ndata. This is particularly suitable when analysis and storage remain\nsmall and no long-running specialised processes are required. Memory and\nprocessing time limits may become problematic with large files.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"option-b-a-managed-vps\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Option B: A managed VPS<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → Rented VPS → Own backend → Own database<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>You have greater freedom regarding programming languages, background\nprocessing and libraries. In return, you need maintenance, firewall\nrules, monitoring and a reliable backup process – unless these tasks are\nexplicitly covered by the managed service contract. A more powerful\nindividual machine could later be supplemented by additional\ninstances.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"option-c-paas-plus-a-managed-database\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Option C: PaaS plus a\nmanaged database<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser → PaaS application → Managed database\n                    │\n                    └── Optional worker / job service<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The platform takes care of launching the application and part of its\noperational management. Your code processes requests; the database can\nbe managed as a separate service. This simplifies certain processes but\npotentially increases both the number of subscribed services and\nusage-based costs. Interfaces between several services must be properly\nsecured.<\/p>\n<p><strong>None of the options is automatically superior.<\/strong> If\nthe first option reliably meets actual demand, additional infrastructure\nis not an improvement. A change should be justified by a specific\nbottleneck or new requirement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A second example: From local AI to an online AI\nservice<\/h2>\n<p>A locally available computer with a powerful graphics card can\ngenerate images or videos. As long as you are the only user, there is no\nobligation to turn it into a public cloud service. For a later offering\nto customers, however, accessibility, job queues, security and costs\nbecome relevant.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Customer → Online order / API → Job database\n                                      │\n                                      ▼\n                                    Queue\n                                      │\n                           ┌──────────┴───────────┐\n                           ▼                      ▼\n                     Local AI computer      Rented GPU instance\n                           │                      │\n                           └──────────┬───────────┘\n                                      ▼\n                                  Result store<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The diagram shows a <strong>hybrid processing approach in the general\nsense<\/strong>, not necessarily a formally defined hybrid cloud. The\nfrontend and job management could run on a small rented service. The\nactual AI computation could take place locally or, when additional\ncapacity is needed, on another provider’s computer. Such a system does\nnot need to exist from day one: initially, even a single worker could\nprocess tasks from a simple queue.<\/p>\n<p>Important operational consequences include local connection outages,\nlimited GPU resources, data protection when processing other people’s\nfiles, secure transfer of jobs, deletion periods, model licensing terms,\nsupport and electricity or usage charges. If the local computer is\nswitched off at night, the product would either have to defer jobs or\nprovide alternative computing capacity. Distributed AI processing is\nexamined in greater depth in M4-L1 and M6-L6.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Costs: What is actually billed?<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>billing model<\/strong> is not identical to the technical\nservice model. A VPS may cost a flat monthly amount; a platform may\ncharge by reserved capacity, invocations or processing time. A\nserverless service can be inexpensive at low usage but become more\nexpensive than a fixed server with high or incorrectly configured\nusage.<\/p>\n<p>Typical cost items include:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cost type<\/th>\n<th>Typical cause<\/th>\n<th>Often overlooked<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Base capacity<\/td>\n<td>Running server, platform instance, database<\/td>\n<td>Possible even during hours of low usage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Computing time<\/td>\n<td>CPU or GPU execution, function invocations<\/td>\n<td>Long jobs, repeated unsuccessful attempts<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Storage<\/td>\n<td>Databases, object storage, snapshots<\/td>\n<td>Old versions, backup retention<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network traffic<\/td>\n<td>Outbound data, cross-region transfer<\/td>\n<td>Downloads and inter-service communication<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Additional services<\/td>\n<td>Logging, monitoring, message brokers<\/td>\n<td>Many small separately billed services<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operations<\/td>\n<td>Maintenance, troubleshooting, customer support<\/td>\n<td>Your own working time is not free<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Thought experiment, not current market prices:<\/strong> If a\nfixed server costs 12 euros per month and usage-based processing for the\nsame demand costs 4 euros, the second option appears more attractive.\nHowever, if daily job volumes increase until the usage-based amount\nreaches 60 euros, a fixed server may be more economical – provided it\ncan handle the load. In practice, additional costs such as databases,\nstorage, data transfer, availability and support must be included.<\/p>\n<p>“Free allowances” also have usage limits and conditions; they must\nnot be planned as permanently free production capacity. A sensible\noperating model requires budgets, warning thresholds, hard limits where\navailable and a clear statement of what happens in cases of misuse or\nunexpected growth. An API without access limits can cause technical and\nfinancial damage even though the cloud provider is functioning\ncorrectly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security, data protection, resilience and dependencies<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"security-and-data-protection\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security and data protection<\/h3>\n<p>A rented platform does not automatically make the processing of\npersonal data compliant with data protection requirements. Relevant\nfactors include storage location, data processing agreements,\nsubprocessors, access permissions, deletion, logging and, where\napplicable, international data transfers. Choosing a European data\ncentre region can be helpful but does not replace assessing the entire\nprocessing chain. These are technical points to check, not binding legal\nadvice.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"resilience\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Resilience<\/h3>\n<p>A service running in a professional data centre can still fail.\nTypical causes include unsuccessful updates, software bugs,\nconfiguration changes, exhausted database connections, faulty\ndependencies or disruptions at a service provider. A provider’s promise\nof high availability does not mean that your entire application has the\nsame availability. Where several dependent services are involved, the\nentire chain must be considered.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"vendor-dependency\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Vendor dependency<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Vendor lock-in<\/strong> means that switching to another\nplatform becomes technically or economically costly. The risk increases,\nfor example, through proprietary interfaces, special data formats or\ntightly interconnected platform services. Open standards, exportable\ndata, documented interfaces and a simple software architecture can\nreduce the risk. However, complete vendor independence is not free:\nadditional layers of abstraction also create work.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"recovery\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recovery<\/h3>\n<p>Regardless of the hosting model, there must be an answer to this\nquestion: <em>What do we do if data disappears or the infrastructure can\nno longer be used?<\/em> Backups, separate retention and occasional\nrecovery tests are distinct from the actual rental of servers. This is\ndiscussed in detail in M3-L6.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision aid: Which model is suitable for which\npurpose?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Starting situation<\/th>\n<th>Usually consider first<\/th>\n<th>Why<\/th>\n<th>What is often unnecessary<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Static information site<\/td>\n<td>Static hosting / traditional web host<\/td>\n<td>Few moving parts<\/td>\n<td>Own VPS or cluster<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small PHP web application<\/td>\n<td>Shared or managed hosting<\/td>\n<td>Existing runtime and database<\/td>\n<td>Complex cloud orchestration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom specialised software / background services<\/td>\n<td>Managed VPS, VPS or PaaS<\/td>\n<td>Greater freedom for processes<\/td>\n<td>Own data centre hardware<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Irregular individual processing tasks<\/td>\n<td>FaaS or job platform<\/td>\n<td>Flexible for suitable workload patterns<\/td>\n<td>Large permanent server<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many independent container services<\/td>\n<td>CaaS / managed container platform<\/td>\n<td>Consistent deployment and coordination<\/td>\n<td>Often excessive for one small service<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Larger database with little time for administration<\/td>\n<td>Managed database<\/td>\n<td>Reduces operational and maintenance work<\/td>\n<td>Own high-availability solution without need<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sensitive data to be processed locally<\/td>\n<td>Self-hosting or suitable isolated solution<\/td>\n<td>Control over data flows<\/td>\n<td>Public upload without a functional reason<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom AI job service<\/td>\n<td>Small backend plus suitable workers<\/td>\n<td>Separate computation from user access<\/td>\n<td>Immediate distributed GPU cluster<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This table provides <strong>questions for orientation<\/strong>, not\nblanket recommendations. Specific requirements determine the choice,\nsuch as data volume, concurrent users, programming environment, legal\nrequirements and available operational expertise.<\/p>\n<p>A proven sequence for making decisions is:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>What function should be offered?<\/strong> Website,\napplication, API or computationally intensive job?<\/li>\n<li><strong>What does it actually need technically?<\/strong> Specific\nruntime, background process, GPU, file storage, database?<\/li>\n<li><strong>What must work securely and reliably?<\/strong> User\naccounts, backups, accessibility, deletion?<\/li>\n<li><strong>What can and do I want to operate myself?<\/strong> Updates\nand administration or a managed service instead?<\/li>\n<li><strong>What ongoing costs arise from low, normal and unexpectedly\nhigh usage?<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>How can I get out again?<\/strong> Data export, switching\nproviders, fallback and recovery plan?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The most important distinction: Hosting model versus your\nown offering<\/h2>\n<p>You can <strong>sell a SaaS application<\/strong> even if, as the\noperator, you use only a VPS. You can <strong>offer an API as a\nservice<\/strong> even if it consists of a single PHP file on shared\nhosting. You can <strong>run an internal AI workflow<\/strong> on your\nown GPU without using any cloud product. And you can <strong>purchase\nSaaS yourself<\/strong> to organise your business without operating any\ninternet application of your own.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Your customer\n   │\n   │ Purchases your software functionality (business model: e.g. SaaS)\n   ▼\nYour application\n   │\n   │ Needs an execution environment (hosting decision)\n   ▼\nShared hosting / VPS / PaaS / Serverless / Own computer\n   │\n   └── Provider takes on different operational tasks depending on the contract<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This distinction is particularly valuable if you are thinking about\nfuture sources of income. <strong>Technical feasibility is not the same\nas customer demand, and scalable infrastructure is not yet a viable\nbusiness model.<\/strong> Only a concrete problem and paying users\njustify additional operational effort.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Self-hosting<\/strong> provides considerable control, but\nentails responsibility for hardware, maintenance and outages.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Colocation<\/strong> moves your own hardware into a data\ncentre, but does not automatically relieve you of administration.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Shared hosting<\/strong> simplifies typical websites;\n<strong>VPS and dedicated servers<\/strong> provide greater freedom and\nusually more operational tasks.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Cloud computing<\/strong> describes on-demand resource\nprovisioning, not simply “computers somewhere else”.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Public, private and hybrid clouds<\/strong> are deployment\nmodels; <strong>IaaS, PaaS and SaaS<\/strong> are different levels of\nservice.<\/li>\n<li><strong>CaaS, FaaS, serverless and managed databases<\/strong> are\nadditional deployment options, not mandatory building blocks of every\napplication.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Shared responsibility<\/strong> means that infrastructure\ntasks can be outsourced; responsibility for your own code, data and use\nof the services remains.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The hosting model and business model are independent of one\nanother.<\/strong> A simply operated online service can be a fully\nfledged product of your own.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Next lesson <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4 –\nVirtualisation and containers<\/a>:<\/strong> What technically is a\nvirtual machine, what does a container do differently, and when is an\norchestration platform such as Kubernetes needed?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Review date: 03.11.2026.<\/strong> This selection includes\nfoundational standards and official vendor and project documentation.\nProduct examples illustrate a service model; specific prices,\ncontractual terms and legal assessments must be checked again for a\nlater real-world decision.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>NIST SP 800-145<\/strong>, <em>The NIST Definition of Cloud\nComputing<\/em> (September 2011): essential cloud characteristics,\nclassic service and deployment models. <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/145/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/145/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud<\/strong>, <em>PaaS versus IaaS versus SaaS\nversus CaaS<\/em> (updated 15.10.2026 according to the page): modern\ndistinctions and example services. <a\nhref=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/paas-vs-iaas-vs-saas\">https://cloud.google.com/learn/paas-vs-iaas-vs-saas<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Microsoft Learn<\/strong>, <em>Shared responsibility in the\ncloud<\/em>: areas of responsibility across self-hosting, IaaS, PaaS and\nSaaS. <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/security/fundamentals/shared-responsibility\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/security/fundamentals/shared-responsibility<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Amazon Web Services<\/strong>, <em>Shared Responsibility\nModel<\/em>: protection of cloud infrastructure and security of one’s own\nuse of the cloud. <a\nhref=\"https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/shared-responsibility-model/\">https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/shared-responsibility-model/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud<\/strong>, <em>Cloud Run overview<\/em>: example\nof a fully managed application and container platform. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run\">https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Amazon Web Services<\/strong>, <em>Understanding the Lambda\nexecution environment lifecycle<\/em>: initialisation and possible cold\nstarts during event-driven execution. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-runtime-environment.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-runtime-environment.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud<\/strong>, <em>Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL – Shared\nResponsibility<\/em>: operator and customer obligations for a managed\ndatabase. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/shared-responsibility\">https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/shared-responsibility<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> The 2011 NIST definition is a deliberately\nstable conceptual foundation; time-dependent statements concerning\nservices were checked against current official provider information.\nProvider prices were not specified.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M2-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M2-L4 – Virtualisation and containers<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor’s: Web, servers and hosting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: How one computer can become several apparently\nindependent computers<\/h2>\n<p>M2-L1 examined how an ordinary computer offers services over the\ninternet. In M2-L2, we divided an application into frontend, backend and\ndata storage. M2-L3 showed who can operate the infrastructure required\nfor these components. We now answer the question: <strong>How can a data\ncentre provide many separate customer environments on a single physical\nserver?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The first answer is <strong>virtualisation<\/strong>. It creates\nseveral logically separate computing environments from real hardware.\nThe second answer is <strong>containerisation<\/strong>: instead of\nstarting a complete virtual computer for every application, processes\nare executed in isolation together with the program components they\nneed. Both approaches solve related but not identical problems. In real\ncloud systems they are even frequently combined.<\/p>\n<p>In what follows, we deliberately distinguish four levels:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Physical computer:<\/strong> Processors, main memory, storage\ndevices and network interfaces.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Virtual machine:<\/strong> A complete computing environment\nwith its own operating system kernel.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Container:<\/strong> An isolated running process or process\ngroup that, on Linux, typically shares an operating system kernel with\nother containers.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Orchestration platform:<\/strong> Software that manages many\nsuch application instances, distributes them across computers and\nreplaces them when failures occur.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>An application does not have to manage all four levels itself. A\nrented virtual server can be entirely sufficient; a managed platform\nservice can even hide nearly all underlying layers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The initial problem: One large server, many small tasks<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a data centre has a powerful computer with many processor\ncores and plenty of main memory. A small customer needs only a fraction\nof these resources. If every customer received a separate physical\nserver, many resources would remain unused. Furthermore, hundreds of\ncustomers would require hundreds of physical devices to be purchased,\ncabled, monitored and replaced.<\/p>\n<p>Different customers’ programs could be installed directly on the same\noperating system. This would be technically possible to some extent, but\nwould lead to conflicts: one program needs a particular runtime library,\nwhile another needs a different one. Different administrators should not\nsee the same files. A crashed service should affect other customers as\nlittle as possible. Nor should changes to one customer’s operating\nsystem affect everyone else.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Virtualisation solves the problem of division and separation\nhere.<\/strong> The customer receives an environment that behaves like\nits own computer, even though it shares physical hardware with other\nenvironments.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-architecture-one-computer-three-virtual-servers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nOne computer, three virtual servers<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Physical server in the data centre\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Processors · RAM · SSD · Network         │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Hypervisor / virtualisation layer        │\n├────────────┬────────────┬────────────────┤\n│ Virtual    │ Virtual    │ Virtual        │\n│ machine A  │ machine B  │ machine C      │\n│ Linux      │ Linux      │ Windows        │\n│ Web service│ Database   │ Specialist app │\n└────────────┴────────────┴────────────────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The diagram is schematic: the actual allocation of resources is\ncontrolled by the hypervisor and the host system. Virtual machines do\nnot automatically receive exclusively dedicated physical processor\ncores.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hypervisor: The manager of virtual machines<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>hypervisor<\/strong> is the central virtualisation software\nor layer. It provides virtual machines with abstracted hardware and\nsupervises their execution on the real hardware. It can, for example,\nallocate memory areas, distribute processor time and connect virtual\ndevices to physical resources.<\/p>\n<p>Two basic types are often distinguished:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type 1 (“bare metal”):<\/strong> The hypervisor operates very\nclose to the hardware. Examples of corresponding platforms include\nMicrosoft Hyper-V and VMware ESXi. Some systems also have a particularly\nprivileged management or host partition.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Type 2 (“hosted”):<\/strong> Virtualisation software runs on\na conventional host operating system, for instance as a desktop\napplication. Oracle VirtualBox is a well-known example.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>This classification is a useful guide, but not a complete technical\ntaxonomy. <strong>KVM<\/strong> (<em>Kernel-based Virtual Machine<\/em>),\nfor example, is a virtualisation feature of the Linux kernel; together\nwith further components, it forms a complete virtualisation platform.\nDifferent architectures do not always fit neatly into the two simplified\ncategories.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"mini-architecture-host-hypervisor-guest\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nHost, hypervisor, guest<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Physical hardware\n       │\n       ▼\nHypervisor / virtualisation environment\n       │\n       ├── Guest operating system A → Applications A\n       └── Guest operating system B → Applications B<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>guest operating system<\/strong> is an operating system\nrunning inside a virtual machine. The <strong>host<\/strong> is the\ncomputer or supporting environment that provides this machine.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"what-problem-does-this-solve\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What problem does this solve?<\/h3>\n<p>The hypervisor enables separate operating systems and resource\nmanagement on the same hardware. It can help to start, shut down,\nmigrate and back up customer systems independently. This is particularly\nuseful for hosting providers, enterprise servers, test environments and\napplications with different operating system requirements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> If a single application\nalready runs reliably on your own computer and separate operating\nsystems are not needed, an additional VM does not necessarily bring any\nbenefit. Instead, it entails its own memory requirements, operating\nsystem updates and administration work.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which parts of a computer are virtualised?<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"virtual-cpu-vcpu\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Virtual CPU (vCPU)<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>vCPU<\/strong> (<em>virtual Central Processing Unit<\/em>,\nvirtual processor) is a computational execution unit presented to the\nvirtual machine. It is not automatically identical to an exclusively\nallocated physical processor core. Several VMs can share processor time.\nTherefore, without knowing the contract, “four vCPUs” on a rented server\ndoes not mean that four real cores are always exclusively available.<\/p>\n<p>A hypervisor coordinates the execution of virtual processors on the\navailable hardware cores. Modern processors support virtualisation\nthrough special hardware features. This reduces the overhead of many\noperations, but does not eliminate it entirely.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"virtual-main-memory\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Virtual main memory<\/h3>\n<p>The virtual machine receives a configured memory area. The hypervisor\nor host system ensures its mapping and isolation. A VM can also use\nvirtual memory and swap files within its own operating system. Thus,\n<strong>a process’s virtual address space<\/strong> (the subject of\nM1-L2) should not be confused with <strong>a virtual\nmachine<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"virtual-storage-devices\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Virtual storage devices<\/h3>\n<p>A VM can see a storage device that is actually provided by a file, a\nlogical volume or a network storage system. The VM often treats it like\nits own disk. Storage devices can grow dynamically; however, shared\nstorage introduces performance limits. A fast virtual server can become\nslow when the physical storage system is under heavy load.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"virtual-network-interfaces\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Virtual network interfaces<\/h3>\n<p>Virtual network cards connect VMs to a virtual switch and, depending\non the configuration, to a physical network. The same basic ideas apply\nas in real networks: addressing, forwarding, firewall rules and\nsegmentation. The fact that machines are virtual does not make them\nautomatically publicly accessible or adequately protected.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"mini-architecture-network-connection\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture: Network\nconnection<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Internet\n   │\nHosting provider’s router / firewall\n   │\nVirtual network switch\n   ├── VM A: Public web frontend\n   └── VM B: Internal database (no public port)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The separation is an <strong>architectural objective<\/strong>, not an\nautomatic property of every installation. Network configuration and\naccess rules must determine which VM may communicate with which other\nVM.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The virtual machine as a fully independent operating\nenvironment<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>virtual machine (VM)<\/strong> usually has its own guest\noperating system, including its own operating system kernel. This kernel\nhandles processes, file systems, memory management and networking\nfunctions inside the VM, for example. A guest can have a different\noperating system from the host, insofar as the architecture,\nvirtualisation platform and permissions support this.<\/p>\n<p>This is one of the most important differences from conventional Linux\ncontainer technology: <strong>A Linux VM runs with its own Linux kernel;\nan ordinary Linux container uses the kernel of its Linux\nhost.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>With VMs, typical tasks therefore arise repeatedly: system\ninstallation, updates, configuration, monitoring, resource planning,\nprotection of administrative access and data backups. In return, the\nseparation offers a comparatively strong security boundary. It is not\nimpenetrable: hypervisor vulnerabilities, misconfigurations, shared data\nareas or network faults can still have an impact.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"starting-copying-snapshots-and-backups\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting, copying,\nsnapshots and backups<\/h3>\n<p>It is convenient to manage VMs as files or defined resources. Some\nplatforms allow the state of a machine to be captured as a\n<strong>snapshot<\/strong> or the machine to be migrated to other\nhardware.<\/p>\n<p>However, a snapshot is <strong>not automatically a reliable\nbackup<\/strong>. The snapshot may depend on the same storage and\nmanagement environment. It may also capture a running database in a\nstate from which reliable recovery requires additional consistency\nmeasures. Important data requires separate backups and tested\nrestoration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why were containers developed?<\/h2>\n<p>Virtual machines isolate entire operating systems. This is valuable,\nbut is often more than a small application requires.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose a web service requires PHP, a particular library version and\nsome configuration files. The operator wants to run it in as similar a\nmanner as possible on a development computer, a test server and later at\na cloud provider. Without additional measures, runtime versions, system\nlibraries and settings may differ. The well-known phrase “it works on my\ncomputer” describes this problem.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Containerisation primarily solves the problem of providing a\nreproducible, isolated application runtime.<\/strong> The application and\nits required files are described as a deployable package and then\nstarted in a controlled environment. Linux uses kernel mechanisms, among\nother things, to isolate processes and limit resources. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-a-container/\">https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-a-container/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-architecture-three-applications-one-kernel\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nThree applications, one kernel<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Physical or virtual Linux server\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Shared Linux kernel                        │\n├──────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┤\n│ Container A  │ Container B  │ Container C  │\n│ PHP app      │ API worker   │ Web server   │\n│ Libraries    │ Libraries    │ Library      │\n└──────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The containers are not three complete Linux computers. They use\nseparate process environments but share the same underlying kernel. The\nillustration also does <strong>not<\/strong> mean that every container is\nincapable of communicating with other containers; desired communication\ncan be configured explicitly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What technically is a container?<\/h2>\n<p>In the conventional Linux context, a <strong>container<\/strong> is\nnot a separate hardware machine but an isolated running process or\nprocess group with a defined view of processes, files, networks and\nother resources.<\/p>\n<p>Two important Linux technologies are:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Namespaces:<\/strong> They give processes their own view of\ncertain areas of the system, such as processes, networks, file system\nmount points and user identifiers. Consequently, a program cannot simply\nsee all processes on the host.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Control groups (cgroups):<\/strong> They organise and limit\nthe use of certain resources, such as processor time, main memory and\nthe number of processes.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Additional security mechanisms such as restricted kernel\ncapabilities, system call filters and less privileged users can also be\napplied. These layers increase security, but their effectiveness depends\non configuration and environment. The official Docker documentation\nexplicitly describes the importance of namespaces, cgroups and\nprotecting the Docker daemon. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/\">https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> Containers are not equivalent to VMs and\ndo not guarantee an equivalent level of isolation. A kernel bug or\nexcessively broad container privileges can endanger the host or other\ncontainers. In particular, the choice of isolation is crucial when\nexecuting untrusted code.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Container image, container and registry: Three different\nthings<\/h2>\n<p>In everyday usage, these terms are frequently confused.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Container image:<\/strong> A packaged, essentially immutable\nset of files, program code, libraries and configuration information. An\nimage is a <strong>template<\/strong>, not yet a running program. Images\nfrequently consist of several reusable layers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Container:<\/strong> A running or stopped instance created\nfrom an image. Several separate container instances can be started from\nthe same image. Each instance typically has its own writable layer.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Registry:<\/strong> A service for storing and distributing\nimages. Other computers can retrieve published images from it.\nWell-known examples include Docker Hub and other private or public\ncontainer registries.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-architecture-from-development-to-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nFrom development to service<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Source code + dependencies\n            │\n            ▼\n        Build image\n            │\n            ▼\n       Container registry\n            │ Retrieve image\n            ▼\n       Production server\n            │\n        Start container\n            │\n            ▼\n     Publicly accessible API<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>When source code is changed, it is sensible to create a new image and\nreplace the existing instance. This avoids manual changes within running\ncontainers that are difficult to trace.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Version identification:<\/strong> Images can be referenced\nthrough a tag (such as a version label) or a cryptographic content\nidentifier (<em>digest<\/em>). A tag may later point to a different\nimage; a content-based digest enables more precise identification of the\npackage used. Neither replaces checks of origin and security,\nhowever.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"what-does-portable-mean\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does “portable” mean?<\/h3>\n<p>An image makes an application environment easier to transfer,\n<strong>but not universally executable everywhere<\/strong>. Processor\narchitecture, operating system kernel, required devices, GPU drivers,\npermissions, networks and file paths can differ. Linux containers\nrequire a compatible Linux kernel; Windows and macOS use a Linux VM for\ncommon Linux containers in Docker Desktop. The idea that “one package\nruns on every computer without any adjustment” is therefore too\nsimplistic. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/learn/content/what-is-a-container/\">https://docs.docker.com/learn/content/what-is-a-container/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Docker: A tool, not container technology itself<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Docker<\/strong> is a widespread ecosystem of tools and\nsoftware for building, distributing and running containers. The name\ndoes not refer to the entire technical standard, nor necessarily to the\nruntime of every container in a cloud.<\/p>\n<p>The Docker ecosystem includes, among other components:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Docker Engine:<\/strong> Components for managing and starting\ncontainers on a host, including the Docker service\n(<em>daemon<\/em>).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dockerfile:<\/strong> A declarative recipe describing how an\nimage is built.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Docker Hub:<\/strong> A container image registry operated by\nDocker.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Docker Compose:<\/strong> A tool that describes and operates\nmultiple related container services, their networks and data storage\ntogether.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The term <strong>Open Container Initiative (OCI)<\/strong> refers to\nan independent standardisation initiative for container image formats,\nexecution and distribution. Container technology is therefore not tied\nto a single tool. Other runtimes and platforms can also use compatible\nformats. <a\nhref=\"https://opencontainers.org/\">https://opencontainers.org/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p>A small example without a configuration file: a web application\nconsists of an HTTP web server, an API process and a database. Docker\nCompose can describe the three services and their communication\ntogether. For an individual server, this is often sufficient already –\n<strong>no Kubernetes cluster is required<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-architecture-a-small-application-with-compose\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nA small application with Compose<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>VPS or local Linux computer\n          │\n     Docker / Compose\n          │\n   ┌──────┼─────────┐\n   │      │         │\n Web server API    Database\n   │      │         │\n   └──────┴─────────┘\n       Internal network\n          │\n        External access\n        to web server only<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This example is an architectural idea, not a mandatory configuration.\nA database can also run as a managed service outside this machine, which\ncan simplify operations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The frequently overlooked point: Where does the data\ngo?<\/h2>\n<p>An important characteristic of many container workflows is the\n<strong>replaceability of application instances<\/strong>. A faulty\ncontainer is removed and a new one started from the verified image. For\nstateless services, this is relatively simple.<\/p>\n<p>It becomes more difficult if a container stores persistent data only\nin its own writable file layer. If this container is deleted, these data\nare usually lost along with that layer. <strong>Restarting the same\ncontainer is not the same as deleting and replacing it.<\/strong> The\nlifecycle stages must be distinguished correctly.<\/p>\n<p>The solution is to deliberately store data <strong>outside the\nshort-lived container layer<\/strong>. Docker supports\n<strong>volumes<\/strong> (managed persistent storage areas) and\n<strong>bind mounts<\/strong> (mounting a particular host directory), for\nexample. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/engine/storage\">https://docs.docker.com/engine/storage<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-architecture-replaceable-application-persistent-data\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nReplaceable application, persistent data<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>          Container: API\n          (replaceable)\n               │\n               ▼\n         Persistent volume\n               │\n          Database files\n               │\n               ▼\n        Separate offline backup<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A volume does <strong>not automatically protect data from drive\nfailure, accidental deletion, ransomware or erroneous database\nchanges<\/strong>. It does not replace a backup. Production databases\nalso require consistent backup procedures, access permissions,\nmonitoring and recovery tests. We examine this subject in greater depth\nin M3-L6.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparison: Virtual machine and container<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>Virtual machine<\/th>\n<th>Typical Linux container<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What is isolated?<\/td>\n<td>Complete virtual computing environment<\/td>\n<td>Application process and view of resources<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Its own kernel?<\/td>\n<td>Usually yes, in the guest operating system<\/td>\n<td>No, the host kernel is shared<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operating systems<\/td>\n<td>Different guest operating systems possible<\/td>\n<td>Kernel compatibility with host required<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Startup and resource overhead<\/td>\n<td>Usually greater because of guest operating system<\/td>\n<td>Usually lower; depends on application<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security boundary<\/td>\n<td>Generally stronger separation<\/td>\n<td>Isolation through shared kernel and configuration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Updates<\/td>\n<td>Guest operating system and application<\/td>\n<td>Host kernel, runtime and new images<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portability<\/td>\n<td>Bound to virtualisation/hardware characteristics<\/td>\n<td>Image easier to distribute, but not universal<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical use<\/td>\n<td>VPS, separate customers, different operating systems<\/td>\n<td>Reproducible application deployment, workers, APIs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The additional resource and administration cost is frequently\nreferred to as <strong>overhead<\/strong>: a VM typically causes more\noverhead because of its own guest operating system, while containers are\nnot free of cost either and entail storage, network and monitoring\ncosts, for example.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000054_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two parallel stacks of layers show containers sharing a host operating system and a virtual machine with its own guest operating system.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two parallel stacks of layers show\ncontainers sharing a host operating system and a virtual machine with\nits own guest operating system.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Containers and virtual machines differ particularly in terms of the\nkernel and guest OS.<\/p>\n<p>The table describes the <strong>usual<\/strong> cases. Special forms\nexist, such as highly isolated container runtimes, microVMs and\ncombinations of both approaches. No absolute guarantee of security or\nspeed can therefore be inferred from a general table.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"common-combination\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Common combination<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Data centre / physical host\n               │\n            Hypervisor\n               │\n          Virtual machine\n               │\n            Linux kernel\n               │\n       Container A + B + C<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This combination is very common in hosting and cloud environments:\nthe customer rents a VM and operates several containers on it. This\nprovides both separation from the physical host and consistent\napplication deployment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does orchestration mean?<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose an API runs not just once but in numerous instances on\nseveral computers. Some computers fail, while others are added. Updates\nshould be carried out gradually. User requests must be distributed among\navailable healthy instances. Simply being able to start individual\ncontainers is no longer sufficient.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Orchestration<\/strong> in this context means coordinated\nmanagement of many running application instances and their dependencies.\nTypical tasks include placement, restarting failed processes, deploying\nnew versions, maintaining the desired number of instances, configuration\nand network accessibility.<\/p>\n<p>One important form is the <strong>declarative operating\nmodel<\/strong>: you describe the state the system should have rather\nthan prescribing every individual step manually. The platform\ncontinually compares actual and desired state and attempts to resolve\ndifferences.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-three-instances-instead-of-two\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Three instances\ninstead of two<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Desired state: 3 API instances\n                │\n                ▼\n         Platform checks\n                │\n    Only 2 instances actually present\n                │\n                ▼\n       Start another instance\n                │\n                ▼\n      Desired state restored<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>However, this only answers part of the reliability question: if an\napplication delivers incorrect results or corrupts a database, starting\nan extra container does not fix the error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Kubernetes: Managing containers in a cluster<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Kubernetes<\/strong> (often called <strong>K8s<\/strong>,\nbecause there are eight letters between K and s) is an open-source\nplatform for operating containerised applications. The name does not\nstand for a specific cloud provider, and Kubernetes is not a complete\nsubstitute for your own development and operational decisions. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000080_en.png\"\nalt=\"Kubernetes cluster with a client, service and three API pods on two worker nodes. A separate control plane schedules and monitors the pods; the application data path leads from the service to the pods.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Kubernetes cluster with a client, service\nand three API pods on two worker nodes. A separate control plane\nschedules and monitors the pods; the application data path leads from\nthe service to the pods.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Simplified Kubernetes topology: control plane for management, service\nfor accessibility and pods on worker nodes. No guarantee of security or\navailability.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>cluster<\/strong> consists of one or more computers called\n<strong>nodes<\/strong>, and a <strong>control plane<\/strong> that\nmanages desired and actual system state. The nodes can be physical or\nvirtual machines.<\/p>\n<p>Some important terms:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pod:<\/strong> The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. A\npod contains one or a few tightly coupled containers with a shared\nnetwork context and, where applicable, shared storage.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Deployment:<\/strong> Description of a desired operational\nstate for normally replaceable application pods, such as instance count\nand image version. Kubernetes manages the associated updates and\nreplicas.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Service:<\/strong> A stable abstraction through which\napplication instances can become accessible within the cluster, even if\nindividual pods are replaced.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Scheduler:<\/strong> A component of the control plane that\ndecides which suitable node should host new pods.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Container runtime:<\/strong> Software on a node that actually\nstarts containers. Kubernetes does not require every node to use Docker\nEngine as its runtime.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"mini-architecture-a-kubernetes-cluster\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture: A\nKubernetes cluster<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>            Incoming requests\n                    │\n               Service access\n                    │\n                Kubernetes\n     ┌──────────────┴─────────────┐\n     │       Control plane        │\n     │ Desired state, management  │\n     └──────────────┬─────────────┘\n                    │ Schedules and controls\n           ┌────────┴────────┐\n           ▼                 ▼\n        Node 1             Node 2\n       ┌───────┐          ┌───────┐\n       │Pod:API│          │Pod:API│\n       │Pod:API│          │Pod:API│\n       └───────┘          └───────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The diagram explicitly does <strong>not illustrate a complete\nproduction high-availability installation<\/strong>. This would require\nseparate planning of the control plane architecture, cluster state\nstorage, network infrastructure, failure scenarios and backups, among\nother things.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"what-kubernetes-actually-makes-easier\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What Kubernetes actually\nmakes easier<\/h3>\n<p>With suitable configuration, Kubernetes can replace failed instances,\ndistribute applications across available nodes, coordinate updates and\nadjust the number of instances. The official documentation mentions\nself-healing mechanisms, declarative control and horizontal scaling,\namong other features. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>What it does not automatically do:<\/strong> Write correct\napplications, ensure the security of every configuration, select a\nbackup strategy, ensure business-level data consistency, perform\ndatabase migrations, ensure data-protection-compliant operations or\ncontrol the cloud bill. Nor is a restart equivalent to actually\nresolving a fault.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"why-is-kubernetes-often-unnecessary-for-small-applications\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why\nis Kubernetes often unnecessary for small applications?<\/h3>\n<p>A single online tool with a manageable user base may only need one\napplication and one database. A managed platform or simple VPS is often\neasier to operate for this purpose. Kubernetes introduces additional\nmanagement objects, networking concepts, roles, log collection, secrets\nmanagement, updates and possible failure scenarios.<\/p>\n<p>It can be worthwhile if many services are operated independently,\nteams frequently deploy new versions or several computers and failure\nzones need to be coordinated. <strong>A platform’s complexity should be\njustified by an actual need, not its popularity.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three specific application scenarios<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-a-small-php-web-application\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario A: Small PHP web\napplication<\/h3>\n<p>You operate a manageable web application with user data and a simple\ndatabase.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Possible solution:<\/strong> Traditional web hosting or a\nsmall VPS with a web server and database. A container is not a\nprerequisite here. Docker Compose could become useful if you wish to\nversion runtime components independently of the host system. A cluster\nplatform would usually create additional work without a corresponding\nbenefit.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational consequences:<\/strong> Security updates, user\npermissions, regular database backups, error logging and occasional\nrecovery tests remain necessary. Inexpensive hosting does not make\nresponsibility for personal data irrelevant.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-b-analysis-api-with-background-jobs\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario B:\nAnalysis API with background jobs<\/h3>\n<p>The application accepts files, provides an <strong>API<\/strong>\n(<em>Application Programming Interface<\/em>) and starts analysis jobs in\nthe background.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Possible solution:<\/strong> A single virtual server operates\na web service, a job queue and one or more workers. These can run in\nseparate containers. As the load increases, the number of workers can be\nincreased separately. A database should store its data outside\nshort-lived container layers and be reliably backed up.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational consequences:<\/strong> CPU and memory\nutilisation, failed jobs, duplicate processing, limits on concurrent\nresource consumption and user permissions become relevant. More workers\ndo not automatically increase database performance; the next bottleneck\nmay be storage or a shared resource.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-c-ai-processing-on-a-gpu\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario C: AI processing on\na GPU<\/h3>\n<p>An AI service accepts tasks, generates images or processes language\nand returns results. A <strong>GPU<\/strong> (<em>Graphics Processing\nUnit<\/em>) is helpful for many such computations, but part of the system\n(web server, job management, user permissions) still frequently runs on\nCPUs.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Possible solution:<\/strong> An inexpensive web server accepts\nrequests; a separate GPU computer processes the jobs. The AI worker can\nbe containerised as long as the required GPU drivers, container runtime,\ndevice driver compatibility and computing resources are appropriate. An\norchestrated multi-node solution only becomes interesting when several\nworker computers need to be managed.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User\n  │\n  ▼\nWeb application / API\n  │\n  ▼\nJob queue\n  │\n  ├── GPU worker 1 (container if appropriate)\n  └── GPU worker 2 (optional)\n  │\n  ▼\nResult store → User receives result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Operational consequences:<\/strong> GPU time, graphics memory,\nmodel sizes, large files, concurrent jobs, electricity or cloud usage,\nprotection against misuse and security updates must be considered.\nSeveral GPU containers imply neither several GPUs automatically nor\nguaranteed additional computing power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security, maintenance and economic limits<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Security:<\/strong> A container is not a trusted sandbox\nsimply because of its name. Security measures include verified images,\ntimely updates, least privilege, secrets management, limited network\naccess and the smallest possible attack surface. Docker, for example,\noffers a <em>rootless mode<\/em> in which the service and containers can\nrun without a permanently privileged root daemon; this has its own\nprerequisites and limitations. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/rootless/\">https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/rootless/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Maintenance:<\/strong> A new image with updated libraries must\nbe built, tested and deployed. Container updates do not replace updates\nto the host kernel, hypervisor, container runtime or an external\ndatabase. Administratively, a virtual system remains a real system.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Backups and failures:<\/strong> The image stores application\nand dependencies, but not automatically all changing user data. Database\nvolumes, keys, configurations and external storage services require\ntheir own backup and recovery strategies. A copy of the same container\non the same server does not protect against failure of that server.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Costs:<\/strong> In addition to actual computing power, costs\narise from storage, network traffic, image storage, log data, backups,\noperations, managed service charges and any spare capacity for\nresilience. Depending on the solution, a simple VM may be cheaper than a\nhighly available container platform; blanket price judgements would be\nunreliable without current offers and a workload profile.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Vendor dependency:<\/strong> Standardised images facilitate\nswitching execution environments. Nevertheless, applications often\ndepend on databases, network services, permissions and proprietary cloud\nfeatures. An OCI-compatible image does not automatically make a complex\napplication transferable to a different provider within minutes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A brief conceptual comparison: Four typical decisions<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement<\/th>\n<th>Usually the obvious approach<\/th>\n<th>When more elaborate technology becomes useful<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Make a simple website accessible<\/td>\n<td>Shared hosting or managed web platform<\/td>\n<td>Custom runtime or operating system requirements<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use your own server with administrative privileges<\/td>\n<td>Single VM / VPS<\/td>\n<td>Separation of many customer environments or heavy load<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operate several application components reproducibly<\/td>\n<td>Containers, possibly Compose<\/td>\n<td>Many services and several independent computers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continually coordinate large numbers of application instances<\/td>\n<td>Managed container platform or Kubernetes<\/td>\n<td>Extensive multi-node and automation requirements<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This classification is not a purchasing recommendation. It explains\nthe additional problem each technology solves. <strong>A more advanced\ntool is not automatically the more economical or secure\nsolution.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Virtualisation<\/strong> divides real hardware into logically\nseparate computing environments.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>hypervisor<\/strong> enables virtual machines to be\nexecuted and managed; a VM generally has its own guest operating system\nand kernel.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Containers<\/strong> usually isolate processes and their\nruntime environment, but share a kernel under Linux.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Images<\/strong> are distributable templates;\n<strong>containers<\/strong> are instances created from them;\n<strong>registries<\/strong> store and distribute images.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Docker<\/strong> is an ecosystem of tools;\n<strong>OCI<\/strong> defines open container standards. <strong>Docker\nCompose<\/strong> is one possible simple solution for several components\non one host.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Persistent data<\/strong> must deliberately be stored outside\nreplaceable container layers and backed up separately.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Kubernetes<\/strong> helps manage many containerised\napplications declaratively across nodes; for a small web application it\nis often unnecessarily complex.<\/li>\n<li><strong>VMs and containers are not mutually exclusive.<\/strong> A\ncommon architecture consists of VMs running containers.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Next lesson <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5 –\nCloud networks and application scaling<\/a>:<\/strong> We examine how\nmultiple application instances work together through load balancers,\nprivate networks and caches – and where new bottlenecks and failure\nrisks emerge.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Source review: 03.11.2026.<\/strong> Official technical\ndocumentation was consulted for this lesson. Some concepts and\nmechanisms are stable; specific product features and security\nrequirements may change. No current prices or binding legal statements\nare claimed.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Microsoft Learn<\/strong>, <em>Hyper-V architecture<\/em>:\nHypervisor, virtualised computers and isolation. <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/de-de/windows-server/virtualization/hyper-v/architecture\">https://learn.microsoft.com/de-de/windows-server/virtualization/hyper-v/architecture<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Docker Docs<\/strong>, <em>What is a container?<\/em>:\nContainer concept and distinction from virtual machines. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-a-container/\">https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-a-container/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Docker Docs<\/strong>, <em>What is an image?<\/em>: Images,\nlayers and packaging. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-an-image/\">https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-an-image/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Docker Docs<\/strong>, <em>Docker Engine security<\/em>:\nNamespaces, cgroups and security boundaries. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/\">https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Docker Docs<\/strong>, <em>Storage<\/em>: Persistent volumes,\nbind mounts and the container lifecycle. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/engine/storage\">https://docs.docker.com/engine/storage<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Docker Docs<\/strong>, <em>Docker Compose<\/em>: Interaction\nof several container services. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/compose\">https://docs.docker.com/compose<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Open Container Initiative (OCI)<\/strong>, official project\noverview: Standardised image, runtime and distribution specifications.\n<a\nhref=\"https://opencontainers.org/\">https://opencontainers.org/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Kubernetes<\/strong>, <em>Concepts / Overview<\/em>: Purpose,\noperation and limitations of the platform. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Kubernetes<\/strong>, <em>Components<\/em>: Control plane and\nnode components. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/components/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/components/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Kubernetes<\/strong>, <em>Pods<\/em>: Smallest deployment\nunit, networking and shared resources. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Kubernetes<\/strong>, <em>Deployments<\/em>: Desired number of\ninstances, rollouts and replicas. <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Docker Docs<\/strong>, <em>Rootless mode<\/em>: Less\nprivileged operation and prerequisites. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/rootless/\">https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/rootless/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a> explains public\naccessibility; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2<\/a> covers\napplication components; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>\ndiscusses the hosting model. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a> examines networks and\nscaling in more depth, <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a>\nreliable protection of persistent data and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L6<\/a> operation of distributed AI\nsystems.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M2-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M2-L5 – Cloud networks and application scaling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor’s: Web, servers and hosting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: How does an application grow beyond a single\nserver?<\/h2>\n<p>In M2-L1, we made a computer publicly accessible. M2-L2 explained how\nbrowsers, interfaces, user accounts and background processing work\ntogether. M2-L3 compared hosting models; M2-L4 explained virtual\nmachines and containers. The next question now arises: <strong>What\nhappens when a single server can no longer reliably meet demand – or\nwhen an outage is no longer acceptable?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The obvious answer is more computing power. This may mean using a\nmore powerful server, operating several application instances, caching\nfrequently used content or moving long-running work into queues.\nHowever, these measures are not interchangeable solutions. An additional\nweb server is of little help if the only database blocks all requests. A\ncache speeds up frequently read data but can deliver outdated responses.\nMore instances increase the number of tasks that can be executed\nsimultaneously, but introduce problems involving sessions and shared\nstate.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Scaling<\/strong> here means adapting a system to a change in\nload. <strong>High availability<\/strong> means continuing to provide a\nservice as far as possible despite certain failures. The two are\nrelated, but they are not the same: a powerful single server can handle\nmany requests and still be a single point of failure. A redundant system\ncan survive a server failure even though it is not particularly\nfast.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"mini-architecture-from-one-server-to-several\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nFrom one server to several<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Small application:\n\nBrowser ──► Web server + application ──► Database\n\nExpanded application:\n\nBrowser\n   │\n   ▼\nLoad balancer\n   ├──► Application server A ──┐\n   ├──► Application server B ──┼──► Shared database\n   └──► Application server C ──┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The second diagram does not automatically solve all performance or\nreliability problems. To do that, the servers must share the same data\nstate sensibly, and the individual components must be coordinated with\neach other.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data centre, region and availability zone<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>data centre<\/strong> is primarily a physical location with\ncomputer hardware, power supply, cooling and network connections. Cloud\nproviders often organise their infrastructure into larger geographical\nareas. A <strong>region<\/strong> is a provider-defined geographical\ngroup of locations. An <strong>availability zone (AZ)<\/strong> is a\ndistinct operating environment within a region on certain cloud\nplatforms. The precise physical and technical boundaries vary according\nto the provider: a zone is not necessarily equivalent to a single\nbuilding.<\/p>\n<p>Why do these areas exist? Because it is undesirable to operate every\nserver in the same room. Otherwise, if the power supply, fire\ncompartment or network connection at one location fails, all copies of\nan application could be affected at the same time. If suitable\ncomponents are distributed across several independent zones, the failure\nof one zone can be handled under certain conditions. However, a region\ncan itself experience widespread problems; multiple zones therefore do\nnot guarantee absolute availability.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Region: example region\n│\n├── Zone A\n│     ├── Application instance 1\n│     └── Database node / storage\n│\n└── Zone B\n      ├── Application instance 2\n      └── Database replica / storage<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>When is it useful?<\/strong> For services where failure of an\nindividual location would be particularly damaging. <strong>When is it\nexcessive?<\/strong> For a hobby website, a small internal tool or a\nservice where occasional interruption is acceptable. Additional data\ntransfer, separate resources, more complex database configuration and\nmore demanding recovery after disruptions must be considered.<\/p>\n<p>Location also has practical implications for latency and data\nprotection. An application accessed mainly by European users may benefit\nfrom regional locations. However, the physical location of data alone\ndoes not answer all legal questions regarding backups, remote access or\ndata processing on behalf of others. The specific legal situation must\nbe assessed separately for a later real-world offering.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Virtual private networks: An internal landscape within the\ncloud<\/h2>\n<p>With a single rented server, one can imagine a machine with a public\ninternet address. Once an application consists of a web server,\ndatabase, workers and administration services, not every component\nshould be publicly accessible. An internal network is needed in which\ncertain systems can communicate with one another while other access is\nrestricted.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>virtual private cloud network (VPC)<\/strong> is a logically\nseparated virtual network within a cloud platform. It resembles an\ninternal company network with address ranges, network segments, routes\nand firewall rules. Providers define the details differently, but the\nbasic function is comparable. <strong>A VPC guarantees neither complete\nphysical isolation nor automatic security.<\/strong> Accidentally making\na database public or allowing excessively broad permissions can create\nconsiderable risks even with a private network.<\/p>\n<p>Important components include:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subnet:<\/strong> A distinct IP address range for part of the\nnetwork. Association with regions and zones varies by provider.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Routing:<\/strong> Rules determining which network paths are\nused for which destination addresses.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Firewall:<\/strong> Rules permitting or blocking particular\nconnections.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Gateway:<\/strong> A connection to other networks, such as\nthe internet.<\/li>\n<li><strong>NAT (Network Address Translation):<\/strong> Address\ntranslation, for instance allowing internal systems to make outbound\ninternet connections without having a public IPv4 address of their\nown.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3\nid=\"mini-architecture-public-entry-point-internal-services\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mini-architecture:\nPublic entry point, internal services<\/h3>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000078_en.png\"\nalt=\"Example HTTP request path from the client through the gateway and application service to the database, and back through the service and gateway to the client.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Example HTTP request path from the client\nthrough the gateway and application service to the database, and back\nthrough the service and gateway to the client.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Schematic request/response path through a cloud service. Roles and\nsecurity checks are distributed differently depending on the system.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                          Internet\n                             │\n                        Public access\n                             │\n                   ┌─────────▼─────────┐\n                   │    VPC network    │\n                   │                   │\n                   │   Load balancer   │\n                   │        │          │\n                   │  Web application  │\n                   │        │          │\n                   │     Database      │\n                   │  (internal only)  │\n                   └───────────────────┘<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The aim is not to make all internal systems completely inaccessible.\nThe application must be able to reach its database, but an arbitrary\nvisitor on the internet must not. This distinction is normally\nimplemented through network boundaries <strong>and additionally<\/strong>\nthrough database permissions and authentication. A firewall is therefore\nonly one layer of the security architecture.<\/p>\n<p>For a small website, a single virtual server with a locally bound\ndatabase may be simpler. A virtual cloud network is especially\nworthwhile when several independently operated components exist and\nclear communication boundaries are required.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Load balancer: Distributing incoming requests<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>load balancer<\/strong> accepts requests and forwards them\nto a suitable application server. This solves two problems: a single\nserver does not have to bear the entire load alone, and under certain\nconditions a failed server can be excluded from handling new\nrequests.<\/p>\n<p>A simple distribution scheme could send requests alternately to\nserver A and server B (<em>round robin<\/em>). Real systems can use\nadditional criteria: existing connections, current utilisation,\ngeographical proximity or defined rules for certain paths. A load\nbalancer can operate at different network layers. For web applications,\nan <strong>application load balancer<\/strong> at the HTTP (Hypertext\nTransfer Protocol) layer is often especially illustrative: it can, for\nexample, distribute requests to <code>/api/<\/code> differently from\nrequests to the website.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>100 simultaneous browser requests\n                 │\n                 ▼\n            Load balancer\n             │       │\n       50 requests  50 requests\n             │       │\n          Server A  Server B<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>These figures are a simplified illustration, <strong>not a\nguarantee<\/strong> that the distribution will always be even. Some\nrequests require considerably more work than others, and connections can\nremain open for longer periods.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"health-checks-and-failures\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Health checks and failures<\/h3>\n<p>A load balancer can check its target servers regularly. Such\n<strong>health checks<\/strong> verify, for example, whether an\napplication responds successfully at a defined endpoint. If a server is\njudged unhealthy, it can temporarily be excluded from selection. This\nassumes that other healthy servers are available and that the load\nbalancer itself functions correctly. Furthermore, an endpoint that\nsimply returns “200 OK” does not prove that the entire application or\ndatabase is functioning correctly.<\/p>\n<p>During updates, a server can be removed from the normal flow of\nrequests so that ongoing connections can finish where possible\n(<em>connection draining<\/em>). What is actually possible depends on the\nplatform and protocol. Long-lived WebSocket connections or active file\ntransfers require particular attention.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"difference-from-a-reverse-proxy\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Difference from a reverse\nproxy<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>reverse proxy<\/strong> is a front-facing server that\naccepts requests on behalf of downstream services. It can terminate\nencryption, forward requests, modify headers and cache content. A load\nbalancer can function as a specialised reverse proxy; conversely, not\nevery reverse proxy needs to distribute load across multiple servers. A\nsingle NGINX server in front of a PHP application can already be useful\nas a reverse proxy without any horizontal scaling taking place.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limits:<\/strong> A load balancer is itself an additional\ncomponent with costs, rules, logs, certificates and failure risks.\nManaged cloud products can take care of its redundancy, but this must be\nchecked for the specific service. If all servers use the same overloaded\ndatabase, the bottleneck simply moves there.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>CDN: Bringing content closer to users<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>content delivery network (CDN)<\/strong> is a distributed\nnetwork for delivering content. It maintains suitable files or responses\nat several geographical locations. Frequently used content therefore\ndoes not have to be transferred from the original web server (<em>origin\nserver<\/em>) for every visitor. This can reduce loading times and the\nload on the origin server.<\/p>\n<p>A CDN is well suited, for example, to images, stylesheets, JavaScript\nfiles, videos or documents that remain unchanged for long periods.\nDifferent rules apply to personalised account data. If a shared cache\ndelivered the private response of an authenticated user to another user,\nthis would be a serious data protection and security failure.\n<strong>Caching must therefore deliberately distinguish between publicly\nreusable and individual content.<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Visitor in Berlin ──► Nearby CDN location ──► Image from cache\n                             │\n                             └── On a cache miss:\n                                  Origin server<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>cache hit<\/strong> occurs when the requested content is\npresent in the cache and can be delivered from there. On a <strong>cache\nmiss<\/strong>, the cache must retrieve it from the original source. This\nis not an error, but normal operation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"keeping-content-up-to-date\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Keeping content up to date<\/h3>\n<p>Caches need rules governing how long a response may be used. The HTTP\nheader <code>Cache-Control<\/code> contains various directives for this\npurpose. For example, <code>max-age<\/code> specifies a freshness\nlifetime. <code>no-store<\/code> instructs the cache not to store a\nresponse; <code>no-cache<\/code>, by contrast, does <strong>not<\/strong>\nmean that nothing may be stored, but requires revalidation before reuse.\n<code>private<\/code> indicates a response intended for private caches;\nthese properties must be carefully observed with shared CDN caches.<\/p>\n<p>A typical approach for static files is to use a new filename or\ncontent hash when changes are made. An old image can then remain in the\ncache while a new version is accessible under a different name. Other\noptions include limited validity periods and targeted cache\ninvalidation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> A small regional website\nwith few visitors does not necessarily need a CDN. A CDN can also\nintroduce extra configuration, data protection issues, caching errors\nand vendor dependencies. Furthermore, it does not replace a database or\nautomatically accelerate every dynamic request.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Caching within an application<\/h2>\n<p>A CDN is only one form of caching. A browser, web server, application\nor database can also retain frequently needed results. The shared\nprinciple is simple: <strong>An expensive result is computed or loaded\nonce and then reused as often as possible.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Example: a statistics service needs ten seconds to analyse several\nmillion records for a public overview. If these statistics change only\nevery five minutes, a cached result might suffice. The database then\ndoes not have to perform the same calculation for every page view.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Request for public statistics\n              │\n              ▼\n        Application cache\n           │       │\n          Hit     Miss\n           │       │\n        Result     ▼\n                Database\n                   │\n               Fill cache<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>However, caches introduce new decisions: how is outdated data\nidentified? Who removes the entry after a change? Can a user\naccidentally see another user’s data? What happens if many requests\nsimultaneously attempt to recompute the same expired entry (<em>cache\nstampede<\/em>)? A cache often increases performance, but also increases\nstate complexity.<\/p>\n<p>A separate in-memory service, such as a key-value store, can provide\na shared cache for several application servers. This is useful in large\nsystems. For small applications, simpler methods often suffice:\nwell-designed database queries, appropriate indexes, local caching or\nstatic output of precomputed pages. A cache is not a substitute for a\ncorrect data model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Vertical and horizontal scaling<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000007_en.png\"\nalt=\"Comparison of vertical scaling through greater capacity per server and horizontal scaling through a load balancer with multiple app instances.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of vertical scaling through\ngreater capacity per server and horizontal scaling through a load\nbalancer with multiple app instances.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustration: More app instances increase capacity only with\nsuitable load distribution and architecture. The database, session state\nand shared components must also be considered; horizontal scaling does\nnot guarantee linear performance gains.<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>Vertical scaling (scale up)<\/strong> means making the\nexisting machine more powerful: more main memory, greater CPU capacity\nor faster storage. <strong>Horizontal scaling (scale out)<\/strong> means\nadding instances of the same task. Both methods can be combined.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Property<\/th>\n<th>Vertical: more powerful server<\/th>\n<th>Horizontal: more instances<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Main idea<\/td>\n<td>More resources in one place<\/td>\n<td>Distribute load across several computers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Advantage<\/td>\n<td>Often a simpler architecture<\/td>\n<td>Additional capacity and possible redundancy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limit<\/td>\n<td>Hardware ceiling, single point of failure<\/td>\n<td>Coordination, shared data storage, communication<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical first step<\/td>\n<td>Increase CPU/RAM<\/td>\n<td>Add a second application instance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational effort<\/td>\n<td>Usually lower<\/td>\n<td>Usually higher<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>As usage grows, a small online service may initially need only more\nRAM or an optimised database query. Building a cluster would be\npremature as long as the actual bottleneck has not been identified.\n<strong>Scaling without measurement<\/strong> is often costly\nspeculation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"why-horizontal-scaling-does-not-always-work\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why horizontal\nscaling does not always work<\/h3>\n<p>Not every task can be divided arbitrarily. Suppose two web servers\nsell the last available ticket at the same time. If both independently\nsee a local inventory of “1”, they can sell it twice. The number of\nservers does not help with such a correctness error. Both need\ncoordinated data storage with suitable transactions or another correct\nreservation mechanism. We discuss this in more detail in module M3.<\/p>\n<p>Furthermore, individual computationally intensive tasks do not\nautomatically run faster simply because several servers exist. A program\nmust be meaningfully divisible into independent parts or be able to\nserve several independent jobs simultaneously. Here we distinguish\n<strong>throughput<\/strong> (many jobs per time period) and\n<strong>latency<\/strong> (the waiting time of an individual job).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stateless: Why stateless web servers are easier to\nreplicate<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>stateless service<\/strong> does not need to rely on data\nfrom a previous request still being present on the same machine. Each\nnew request carries the necessary information, or the service retrieves\nit from shared storage. This allows a load balancer to send subsequent\nrequests to any available instance.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>stateful service<\/strong>, by contrast, retains information\nabout earlier processing. This is by no means inherently bad: databases\nare naturally stateful, and long-lived connections have state too. For\nhorizontally scaled web applications, however, it is advantageous not to\nstore especially short-lived state exclusively within a single web\nserver process.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-user-sign-in\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: User sign-in<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser with session cookie\n          │\n          ▼\n      Load balancer\n       /          \\\n  Web server A  Web server B\n       \\          /\n        ▼        ▼\n     Shared session/account data\n                │\n             Database<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>cookie<\/strong> is a small piece of information that a\nbrowser sends with appropriate requests. A server-side <strong>session\nstore<\/strong> can keep the corresponding session data centrally.\nAlternatively, there are approaches using signed tokens; these also\nrequire careful permission checks, secure storage and a revocation plan.\nNeither a cookie nor a token alone makes an application secure.<\/p>\n<p>There are also <strong>sticky sessions<\/strong>, where a load\nbalancer attempts to send a user back to the same server. This can make\nexisting applications easier to scale in the short term. If that server\nfails or is replaced, however, local session information may be lost. In\nthe long term, shared or otherwise correctly managed state is often more\nrobust, although it in turn requires infrastructure and incurs\ncosts.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"sharing-files-and-databases\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sharing files and databases<\/h3>\n<p>A second web server does not automatically see files uploaded locally\nto the first. User uploads must be stored, for example, in shared object\nstorage, a suitable shared file system or another coordinated data\nstore. The same applies to database files: a local SQLite file on server\nA does not automatically become the identical file on server B. Simply\ncopying a live database file is no substitute for correct replication or\ntransaction coordination.<\/p>\n<p>The proven basic design for many web services is therefore\n<strong>replaceable web instances plus explicitly managed shared data\nstorage<\/strong>. The database itself can then become a bottleneck or a\npoint of failure, however. The course therefore turns next to databases,\nreplication and sharding.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Autoscaling: Adapting capacity to demand<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Autoscaling<\/strong> means that a platform provisions\nadditional resources or reduces capacity no longer needed, based on\ndefined rules or metrics. Such a system observes, for example, CPU\nutilisation, the number of simultaneously open requests, response times\nor the length of a queue.<\/p>\n<p>In Kubernetes, a <strong>Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)<\/strong> can\nchange the number of pods – that is, deployed application instances –\nbased on metrics. A separate system can ensure that additional computing\nnodes become available as required. These two processes are not\nidentical: more desired pods do not create additional hardware if the\ncluster has no available space. <strong>A particular feature of the\nKubernetes HPA CPU metric:<\/strong> With the corresponding HPA\nconfiguration, percentage <em>CPU utilisation<\/em> is measured relative\nto the CPU <em>requests<\/em> defined for the containers, not as a\nblanket percentage of an entire host processor. Missing required\nrequests can prevent evaluation of this metric type; HPA also supports\nother resource, custom and external metrics. A value such as “70% CPU\nutilisation” is therefore only meaningful when its measurement reference\nis specified. This detail is described in the <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/autoscaling/horizontal-pod-autoscale/\">Kubernetes\nHPA documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Metrics: Requests / utilisation\n                │\n                ▼\n            Scaling rule\n                │\n       ┌────────┴────────┐\n       ▼                 ▼\n   More demand       Less demand\n       │                 │\n   Instances ↑       Instances ↓<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Example: an online tool receives many requests during the day and\nhardly any at night. The platform could start additional application\ninstances during the day and shut some down at night. This only works if\nstartup times, data access and load measurements are appropriate.\nContainers may have to load images, initialise applications and\nestablish database connections before they can serve any requests at\nall.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Autoscaling is not an immediate guarantee against\noverload.<\/strong> A sudden surge can arrive faster than new instances\nbecome available. Overly aggressive scaling rules can lead to continual\nscaling up and down. Minimum and maximum limits, sensible metrics, spare\ncapacity and a strategy for orderly rejection of additional load are\ntherefore needed.<\/p>\n<p>A CPU measurement alone can be misleading: if an application spends\nmost of its time waiting for a slow database, its CPU utilisation may\nremain low even though users wait a long time. Suitable metrics must\ncorrespond to the actual bottleneck.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rate limits, queues and backpressure: Controlling\noverload<\/h2>\n<p>Not all demand should be passed immediately through to the database\nor AI model. If a system is subjected to more work than it can process,\nthe result is increasing waiting times, full storage or memory, aborted\nrequests and possibly a complete collapse. Three mechanisms help manage\nsuch load.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"rate-limiting-limiting-request-rates\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rate limiting – limiting\nrequest rates<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>rate limit<\/strong> restricts the frequency of particular\nactions within a time period. An API service could, for example, limit\nthe number of requests per user, access key or account. This protects\ninfrastructure and other customers from unintentional overload or\nmisuse. When a limit is exceeded, an HTTP server can return the status\n<strong>429 Too Many Requests<\/strong> and, where appropriate, indicate\nwhen another request would be reasonable.<\/p>\n<p>The specific limit depends on computational costs, fairness, the\ncontractual model and protection requirements. A single IP address is\noften insufficient as an identifier, since legitimate users can share an\naddress. Conversely, multiple accounts or distributed attacks can\ncircumvent a naive IP-based limit. Rate limits are protective rules, not\na complete security strategy.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"queue-accepting-jobs-and-processing-them-later\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Queue –\naccepting jobs and processing them later<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>queue<\/strong> is a waiting line for jobs. Instead of\nexecuting a costly operation throughout the entire browser request, an\napplication can store it as a job. A background process – a\n<strong>worker<\/strong>, or job processor – later retrieves it from the\nqueue and processes it. In the meantime, the website can show the user a\njob identifier and status.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User ──► API ──► Job database / queue\n                            │\n                 ┌──────────┴──────────┐\n                 ▼                     ▼\n              Worker A              Worker B\n                 │                     │\n                 └──────────┬──────────┘\n                            ▼\n                          Result\n                            │\n                       Status query<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This is particularly illustrative for video conversion, data analysis\nor AI image generation. An individual job may take many seconds or\nminutes. It does not have to block the web connection for its entire\nduration. More workers increase potential throughput as long as storage,\nGPUs, the database and other resources can keep up.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> Queues can redeliver jobs if\nacknowledgements are missing or a worker crashes. The application may\nneed to handle the same message safely several times\n(<strong>idempotency<\/strong>). For example, a job must not be billed\ntwice without detection. The exact delivery guarantee depends on the\nqueue used.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"backpressure-signalling-a-limit-upstream\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Backpressure –\nsignalling a limit upstream<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Backpressure<\/strong> describes the principle of not\nsupplying an overloaded downstream component with an unlimited number of\nfurther tasks. In simplified terms: if the production rate exceeds the\nprocessing rate, the system must either slow the inflow, increase\ncapacity, reject tasks in a controlled way or discard them according to\nclear rules.<\/p>\n<p>A queue alone does not solve the problem. If 1,000 jobs arrive per\nminute but only 500 are processed, the queue keeps growing. Additional\nworkers could help as long as they can be used effectively. Otherwise,\nthe application needs admission limits and clear status messages. A “job\naccepted” response should only be issued when reliable storage of the\njob is actually ensured.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How to identify the right bottleneck<\/h2>\n<p>Before deciding on any scaling measure, one should ask <strong>which\nresource is actually slowing the service down<\/strong>. For a web\napplication, there are several possible causes:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symptom<\/th>\n<th>Possible bottleneck<\/th>\n<th>Often a sensible first approach<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Server CPU heavily occupied for a long time<\/td>\n<td>Computation<\/td>\n<td>Inspect code/queries, more powerful CPU or additional instances<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many requests but low CPU utilisation<\/td>\n<td>Database/network waiting times<\/td>\n<td>Investigate slow queries and dependencies<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Large images load slowly<\/td>\n<td>File size/transfer<\/td>\n<td>Optimise files, caching or CDN<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow only with simultaneous use<\/td>\n<td>Resource or lock conflicts<\/td>\n<td>Examine concurrency, connection limits and database access<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long AI job waiting times<\/td>\n<td>Too few workers/insufficient GPU capacity<\/td>\n<td>Assess queue length, runtimes and capacity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sporadic outage when a server fails<\/td>\n<td>Single point of failure<\/td>\n<td>Backups, recovery and redundant instances where appropriate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Metrics<\/strong> (measurable quantities),\n<strong>logs<\/strong> (event records) and, where applicable,\n<strong>traces<\/strong> (tracking a request through several services)\nare useful here. A single number can be misleading. For instance, an\naverage response time can look good even though a few users wait for\nminutes. For usability, high percentiles – such as the slower five per\ncent of requests – are also important.<\/p>\n<p>For small services, simple measures are often sufficient: clear error\nlogs, regular backups, a few comprehensible performance indicators and\noccasional capacity checks. A complete observability system with many\nspecialised services might be excessive here. Operations also mean\nkeeping complexity as low as possible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three end-to-end application examples<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"121-dokumentationswebsite-mit-bildern\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Documentation website\nwith images<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Initial situation:<\/strong> A website publishes articles,\nprogram files and screenshots. Visitors predominantly read and write\nlittle or nothing themselves. This is a favourable case for static\ncontent and caching.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Possible architecture:<\/strong> HTML files and images on a\nconventional web host or small server; optionally, a CDN later for\nlarger, frequently retrieved files. There need not be a database, load\nbalancer or container cluster. Security updates for the software used,\nreliable backups and domain management nevertheless remain\nnecessary.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limit:<\/strong> A complex cloud platform would not improve\nthe actual content. It would become more attractive only for special\nrequirements such as substantial global traffic, costly media processing\nor very high availability objectives.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"122-kleines-kostenpflichtiges-online-analysewerkzeug\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Small paid\nonline analysis tool<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Initial situation:<\/strong> Customers upload data, start\nanalyses and retrieve results. Personal data, customer accounts and jobs\nwith different costs arise.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Possible initial architecture:<\/strong> A web application on\na single VPS (Virtual Private Server), a suitable database and secure,\nbounded upload storage. A small queue with one worker for longer\nanalyses. Roles, access control per customer, limits, backups and\ntraceable billing data are more important than a premature multi-zone\narchitecture.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Growth path:<\/strong> First optimise the program and\ndatabase, then move workers out if appropriate and place several\nstateless web instances behind a load balancer. User data, sessions and\nuploaded files then require shared, correctly secured storage. Automatic\nscaling or redundant databases follow only when there is a specific\nneed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Risk:<\/strong> An accidentally publicly shared result cache\nwould be more dangerous than a slow page. Unrestricted uploads and\ncomputations can also cause costs and storage requirements to increase\nuncontrollably.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"123-ki-verarbeitungsdienst-mit-gpus\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>AI processing service with\nGPUs<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Initial situation:<\/strong> Users initiate image or video\ngeneration with varying runtimes. <strong>GPU<\/strong> means\n<em>Graphics Processing Unit<\/em>, a graphics processor suitable for\nmany parallelisable AI computations. The actual processing is\nconsiderably more expensive than serving a status page.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser\n   │\n   ▼\nLightweight API / sign-in\n   │\n   ▼\nJob acceptance and cost limit\n   │\n   ▼\nQueue\n   │\n   ├──► GPU worker A\n   └──► GPU worker B (as needed)\n             │\n             ▼\n         Result store\n             │\n             ▼\n         Secure delivery<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The web interface may need little computing power, while individual\nAI jobs consume a large proportion of GPU capacity. Two separate scaling\ndecisions are therefore useful: how many web instances are needed, and\nhow many AI workers? GPU startup times, model loading times, memory\nconsumption, waiting times and handling of cancelled jobs must also be\nconsidered.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> If an individual operator\noccasionally generates images locally or tests a small service of their\nown, a local GPU machine or a single continuously running worker can\nsuffice. A cloud cluster is not a prerequisite for AI research or an\ninitial offering.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Operations, costs, security and consequences of\nfailure<\/h2>\n<p>The crucial problem in modern cloud architectures is rarely just\nstarting additional servers. In the long term, all components must be\nmanaged together.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Costs:<\/strong> More instances and zones mean additional\nongoing computing and storage costs. Load balancers, CDNs, public IP\naddresses, managed databases, transfers between zones and outbound data\ntraffic can incur separate charges. A cache or autoscaling can reduce\ncosts, but also increase them if incorrectly configured. Specific\nprovider prices are deliberately not claimed here.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Security:<\/strong> Keep the public entry surface as small as\npossible; do not unnecessarily expose databases and administration\nservices to the internet. Internal connections also require appropriate\nauthentication, authorisation checks, encrypted transfers as needed and\nkey management. Each new service increases the number of potential\nmisconfigurations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Availability:<\/strong> Running several web servers is only\npart of the solution. If the database, session store or load balancer\nremains a single point of failure, the entire service can still fail. A\nfailure plan should consider which data can be rebuilt, what is reliably\nbacked up and how long recovery may take.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Data protection:<\/strong> Logs, caches, uploads, backup\ncopies and CDN locations can contain personal data. Storage, deletion,\naccess protection and, where applicable, processing on behalf of others\nmust be assessed for the specific application. Personal content does not\nbelong in a publicly reusable CDN cache without appropriate\nsafeguards.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Maintenance and support:<\/strong> More services require more\nconfiguration, updates, monitoring and fault diagnosis. For an\nindividual developer in particular, not only the monthly infrastructure\ncharge matters, but also the time available for operations and customer\nsupport.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision rule: From simple to complex<\/h2>\n<p>For a typical small online service, the following sequence is often\nmore reasonable than building a cluster immediately:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Simple working application<\/strong> on a suitable hosting\nservice.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Measure what actually limits performance:<\/strong> CPU, RAM,\nnetwork, database, file access or user behaviour.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Remove obvious bottlenecks:<\/strong> More efficient queries,\nbetter indexes, suitable files, bounded uploads and caching.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Scale vertically<\/strong> as far as this is technically and\neconomically appropriate.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Move slow tasks out<\/strong>, for example into workers using\na queue.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Use several replaceable application instances<\/strong> only\nwhen there is a real load or availability requirement.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Consider automatic scaling and multiple zones<\/strong> for\nservices whose requirements justify additional operational effort.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This is not a rigid rule. A service with life-critical requirements\nneeds different security and availability measures from the outset than\na hobby website. Conversely, a complex distributed architecture is not a\nseal of quality if it solves problems that do not exist.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M3-L1<\/h2>\n<p>The main building blocks can be briefly distinguished from one\nanother:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technology<\/th>\n<th>Main task<\/th>\n<th>Typical misconception<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Region / zone<\/td>\n<td>Geographical and operational separation<\/td>\n<td>Several zones guarantee survival of every failure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VPC<\/td>\n<td>Logically separated network areas<\/td>\n<td>“Private” automatically means secure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Load balancer<\/td>\n<td>Distribute requests across instances<\/td>\n<td>It removes every bottleneck<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CDN<\/td>\n<td>Provide suitable content close to users<\/td>\n<td>All dynamic responses can safely be cached<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cache<\/td>\n<td>Avoid repeated computation/transfer<\/td>\n<td>It always delivers up-to-date data<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertical scaling<\/td>\n<td>Give one machine more resources<\/td>\n<td>Computing capacity grows without limit<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Horizontal scaling<\/td>\n<td>Add further instances<\/td>\n<td>Data and sessions are automatically shared<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Autoscaling<\/td>\n<td>Adjust capacity based on rules<\/td>\n<td>New capacity is immediately available<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rate limit / queue / backpressure<\/td>\n<td>Absorb and control overload<\/td>\n<td>A queue has unlimited processing capacity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>The most important connection:<\/strong> A web application is\neasier to scale horizontally if its short-lived processes remain\nreplaceable and shared data state is deliberately managed. Precisely\nthis data storage becomes the next central technical topic.<\/p>\n<p>M2-L5 completes the area <strong>B – Servers, hosting and internet\napplications<\/strong>. In <strong>M3-L1 – A database on a\nserver<\/strong>, we examine what happens internally during a database\nquery, why indexes are so important, what concurrent writes trigger and\nwhich limits even powerful individual servers face. From there, the path\nleads to replication, database clusters and sharding.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 03.11.2026<\/p>\n<p>The sources serve to verify technical concepts. Vendor documentation\ndescribes the scope of the respective product; it is not a neutral\neconomic assessment. Names and details may change.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Google Cloud – Regions and zones: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/regions-zones\">https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/regions-zones<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Google Cloud – VPC networks: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/vpc\">https://docs.cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/vpc<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Google Cloud – Subnets: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/subnets\">https://docs.cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/subnets<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>AWS – Application Load Balancer, target groups and health checks: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/load-balancer-target-groups.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/load-balancer-target-groups.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>AWS – Health checks for target groups: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/target-group-health-checks.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/target-group-health-checks.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Cloudflare – Introduction to CDN caching (updated 12.10.2026): <a\nhref=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/cache/get-started/\">https://developers.cloudflare.com/cache/get-started/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Cloudflare – Cache concepts: <a\nhref=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/cache/concepts/\">https://developers.cloudflare.com/cache/concepts/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>MDN – <code>Cache-Control<\/code> and cache behaviour: <a\nhref=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/de/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Cache-Control\">https://developer.mozilla.org/de/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Cache-Control<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Kubernetes – Workload autoscaling: <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/autoscaling/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/autoscaling/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>Kubernetes – Horizontal pod autoscaling: <a\nhref=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/autoscaling/horizontal-pod-autoscale/\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/autoscaling/horizontal-pod-autoscale/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li>AWS – Amazon SQS and retries for queued jobs: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/designing-for-outage-recovery-scenarios.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/designing-for-outage-recovery-scenarios.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Scope:<\/strong> Cost and load examples are illustrative\nmodels. No specific currently available offers, guaranteed achievable\navailability levels or legally binding data protection assessments are\nclaimed.<\/p>\n<\/article>"};
